\id PRO - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.7
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Proverbs
\toc1 Proverbs
\toc2 Proverbs
\mt1 Proverbs
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 The purpose of proverbs
\p
\v 1 \add These are\add* the proverbs \add collected\add* by King Solomon of Israel \add King\add* David’s son):
\q1
\v 2 \add These will help you\add* to know wisdom and instruction,
\q1 \add and\add* to comprehend sayings \add that give\add* understanding.
\q1
\v 3 \add Also\add* to learn insight
\q1 \add and to understand\add* what’s right and \add leads to\add* justice and integrity.
\q1
\v 4 \add They\add* give good advice to those who are naive—
\q1 \add teaching\add* knowledge and discretion to young people.
\q1
\v 5 Let wise people \add also\add* listen \add to these sayings\add* to keep learning more,
\q1 and discerning people can acquire guidance—
\q1
\v 6 understanding proverbs and parables—
\q1 the words of wise people plus their challenges.
\s1 The warning to young people
\p
\v 7 Deciding to obey Yahweh is the beginning of knowledge.
\q1 Fools despise wisdom and being told what they should do.
\q1
\v 8 My child, listen to your father’s instructions.
\q1 Don’t reject what your mother has told you to do,
\q1
\v 9 because their teaching is like a graceful garland to wear on your head,
\q1 or like \add beautiful\add* necklaces to wear around your neck.
\p
\v 10 My child, if evil people tempt you \add to join them\add*, don’t agree to it.
\v 11 If they say,
\q1 “Come with us. We’ll find someone to kill—
\q1 we’ll hide and ambush an innocent person \add just for fun\add*.
\q1
\v 12 Let’s swallow innocent people just like the grave does,
\q1 and healthy people just like those going down to the pit.
\q1
\v 13 We’ll find lots of valuable loot.
\q1 We’ll fill our houses with what we take.
\q1
\v 14 You need to come and join us—
\q1 we share everything equally.”
\q1
\v 15 My child, don’t follow their ways.
\q1 Keep your foot away from their path
\q1
\v 16 because their feet run towards evil,
\q1 and they rush to pour out blood.
\q1
\v 17 Surely it would be a waste of time
\q1 to spread out a net while the bird is watching,
\q1
\v 18 but they lie in wait for their own blood—
\q1 they ambush their own lives.
\q1
\v 19 That’s what happens to anyone who gains wealth illegally—
\q1 it takes that person’s life.
\s1 Accepting or rejecting wisdom
\q1
\v 20 Wisdom calls out loudly on the street—
\q1 she makes her voice known in the plazas.
\q1
\v 21 She calls out from above the busy streets.
\q1 She tells her sayings at the meeting place at the city gates.
\q1
\v 22 How much longer will naive people love \add their\add* naivety,
\q1 and mockers enjoy their mockery,
\q1 and stupid people despise knowledge?
\q1
\v 23 Change your ways when I correct you.
\q1 Listen, I’ll pour my spirit out on you all.
\q1 Let me declare my messages to you all
\q1
\v 24 because I’ve called and you’ve all refused \add to listen\add*.
\q1 I held out my hand but no one paid \add any\add* attention
\q1
\v 25 and you all ignored all my advice
\q1 and took no notice when I corrected you.
\q1
\v 26 So I’ll laugh when calamity hits you all
\q1 and I’ll ridicule you when you’re all terrified
\q1
\v 27 when panic strikes you all like a storm
\q1 and calamity comes like a whirlwind
\q1 and when trouble and anguish reach you all.
\q1
\v 28 Then they’ll call out to me \add for help\add* but I won’t answer.
\q1 They’ll search for me but won’t be able to find me
\q1
\v 29 because they hated wisdom
\q1 and didn’t choose to respect and obey Yahweh.
\q1
\v 30 They didn’t listen to my advice.
\q1 They didn’t want to hear whenever I corrected them,
\q1
\v 31 so they’ll eat the fruit of their own chosen path
\q1 and they’ll be filled with their own schemes.
\q1
\v 32 Naive people will get killed \add as a result of their own\add* turning away,
\q1 and the fools’ \add own\add* complacency will destroy them.
\q1
\v 33 However, those who listen to me will have secure lives
\q1 and be able to relax without worrying about troubles \add coming\add*.
\c 2
\s1 The peace/prosperity from wisdom
\p
\v 1 My child, if you accept my sayings
\q1 and internalise my commands,
\q1
\v 2 \add so\add* you’ll be attentive to wisdom
\q1 \add and\add* your mind will look for understanding,
\q1
\v 3 if you’ll call out for insight,
\q1 raising your voice \add to ask\add* for understanding,
\q1
\v 4 if you go after it as if it’s silver
\q1 and search for it as if it was hidden treasure,
\q1
\v 5 then you’ll understand \add how to\add* respect and obey Yahweh
\q1 and you’ll learn more about God.
\q1
\v 6 \add That’s\add* because Yahweh gives wisdom—
\q1 knowledge and understanding come from his mouth
\q1
\v 7 and he gives true wisdom to godly people—
\q1 a \add protective\add* shield for those walking with integrity.
\q1
\v 8 to guard the paths of justice,
\q1 and he will preserve the way of those who are faithful to him.
\q1
\v 9 Then you’ll understand righteousness and justice and integrity—every good path \add to walk\add*,
\q1
\v 10 because wisdom will enter into your mind
\q1 and knowledge will give you pleasure.
\q1
\v 11 Discretion will watch over you.
\q1 Understanding will guard you
\q1
\v 12 to rescue you off the path of wicked people—
\q1 from people who speak perversely—
\q1
\v 13 the ones who abandon the paths of godliness
\q1 to walk in the ways of darkness,
\q1
\v 14 the ones who are glad to do evil.
\q1 They rejoice in perverse things of evil,
\q1
\v 15 whose paths are crooked
\q1 and going astray in their tracks.
\p
\v 16 \add Being discreet and insightful\add* will rescue you from a strange woman—
\q1 from a foreign woman who makes her sayings smooth,
\q1
\v 17 who’s left her husband from her younger days,
\q1 and has forgotten her promise to her god.
\q1
\v 18 Her house sinks down to death,
\q1 and her paths to the departed spirits.
\q1
\v 19 No one who goes into her \add place\add* will return,
\q1 and they won’t find the paths \add that lead to\add* life.
\q1
\v 20 So follow the ways of good \add people\add*,
\q1 and stay on the path of those who want to do what’s right,
\q1
\v 21 because it’s \add people\add* who do what is right who’ll live in \add this\add* land,
\q1 and those who are blameless will remain in it.
\q1
\v 22 However, the wicked \add people\add* will be taken off the land,
\q1 and those who are treacherous will be torn away from it.
\c 3
\s1 Advice to the young
\q1
\v 1 My son, don’t forget my instructions,
\q1 and keep my commands in your thoughts,
\q1
\v 2 →because they’ll give you a longer lifetime
\q1 and add peace to your \add life\add*.
\q1
\v 3 Don’t let loyal commitment and faithfulness leave you:
\q1 tie them around your neck,
\q1 \add and\add* inscribe them into your mind.
\q1
\v 4 Then you’ll find favour and good insight
\q1 in the eyes of God and man.
\q1
\v 5 Trust in Yahweh with everything in you,
\q1 ^and don’t rely on your own logic.
\q1
\v 6 Acknowledge Yahweh’s \add involvement\add* in everything you do,
\q1 and he’ll make the way easier to follow.
\q1
\v 7 Don’t pride yourself on your own wisdom,
\q1 ^\add but rather\add* obey Yahweh and turn away from evil.
\q1
\v 8 It will be healing for your stomach,
\q1 and refreshment for your bones.
\q1
\v 9 Honour Yahweh from your wealth
\q1 and from the first of everything you harvest,
\q1
\v 10 →then your barns will be completely filled,
\q1 and your vats will overflow with new wine.
\q1
\v 11 My son, don’t reject correction from Yahweh,
\q1 and don’t resent his rebuke,
\q1
\v 12 because Yahweh disciplines those he loves,
\q1 just like a father does to his child that he appreciates.
\q1
\v 13 The person who discovers wisdom is blessed,
\q1 as is the one who obtains understanding,
\q1
\v 14 because it’s better than a good income.
\q1 Being wise is of more value than gold.
\q1
\v 15 Wisdom is more valuable than jewelry.
\q1 →There’s nothing else you could want that’s worth as much as being wise.
\q1
\v 16 On one hand, wisdom leads to a longer life.
\q2 On the other hand, being wise will enable riches and honour.
\q1
\v 17 The ways of wisdom are pleasant,
\q1 and all its ways are peaceful.
\q1
\v 18 Wisdom is a tree of life for those who embrace it.
\q1 People who are consistently wise are blessed.
\q1
\v 19 Yahweh founded the world using wisdom.
\q1 He established the atmosphere and sky by understanding.
\q1
\v 20 As a result of his knowledge, the ocean depths broke open,
\q1 and the clouds dripped dew.
\b
\q1
\v 21 My son, don’t let wisdom, knowledge, or understanding escape from your sight.
\q1 Safeguard sound wisdom and discretion
\q1
\v 22 and they’ll liven your mind,
\q1 →and then people will notice it about you.
\q1
\v 23 Then you’ll walk in confidence
\q1 without \add tripping or\add* stumbling.
\q1
\v 24 When you go to bed, your mind won’t be filled with fear.
\q1 You’ll lie down and your sleep will be relaxing.
\q1
\v 25 Don’t worry about something terrible suddenly happening \add to you\add*,
\q1 or about when \add you see\add* wicked people facing ruin,
\q1
\v 26 because your confidence will be in Yahweh,
\q1 and he will protect you on your journey.
\b
\q1
\v 27 Don’t hold back when you’re able to do good to someone—
\q1 when you have the ability and opportunity to act.
\q1
\v 28 Don’t tell your neighbour, “Go home \add now\add*, but come back tomorrow and I’ll give it to you then,”
\q1 when you had it there the whole time.
\q1
\v 29 Don’t plot how to take advantage of your neighbour,
\q1 when he’s living beside you and trusting you.
\q1
\v 30 Don’t quarrel with others for no reason—
\q1 if they haven’t done anything wrong to you.
\q1
\v 31 Don’t envy violent people,
\q1 and don’t imitate how they behave,
\q1
\v 32 because Yahweh detests crooked people
\q1 ^but is friendly towards those who are godly.
\q1
\v 33 Yahweh’s curse is on the household of wicked people,
\q1 ^but he blesses the home of those who do what’s right.
\q1
\v 34 He mocks those who mock others,
\q1 but he shows favour towards those who are humble.
\q1
\v 35 Wise \add people\add* will inherit honour,
\q1 but fools will receive shame.
\c 4
\s1 The teaching of children
\q1
\v 1 My children, listen to your father’s teachings.
\q1 Pay attention so you’ll learn well,
\q1
\v 2 because I’ll give you good teaching.
\q1 Don’t abandon my instructions.
\q1
\v 3 When I was a boy in my father’s house,
\q1 and I was my mother’s tender son,
\q1
\v 4 then he taught me and told me,
\q1 “Remember what I have told you.
\q1 Follow my instructions and live.
\q1
\v 5 Acquire wisdom and understanding.
\q1 Always remember what I’ve told you and keep doing it.
\q1
\v 6 Don’t abandon wisdom and it’ll preserve you.
\q1 Love it, and it will guard you.
\q1
\v 7 Wisdom is the priority—get hold of it,
\q1 and more than anything, acquire understanding.
\q1
\v 8 Esteem wisdom and it will lift you up.
\q1 Wisdom will honour you when you embrace it.
\q1
\v 9 Wisdom will place a wreath of grace on your head.
\q1 It’ll present you with a crown of beauty.
\b
\q1
\v 10 My child, listen to my words and accept them,
\q1 →and that’ll increase the years of your life.
\q1
\v 11 I taught you how to act wisely,
\q1 and I directed you towards the right paths.
\q1
\v 12 When you walk, your steps won’t be hampered,
\q1 and if you run, you won’t stumble.
\q1
\v 13 Take hold of my discipline—don’t let it go.
\q1 Guard it, because it’s your lifeline.
\q1
\v 14 Don’t imitate what wicked people do,
\q1 and don’t go the same way as evil people.
\q1
\v 15 Avoid that path—don’t even walk across it.
\q1 Keep right away from it and go somewhere else,
\q1
\v 16 because they can’t nap unless they’ve done some harm,
\q1 and they’ll be robbed of sleep if they haven’t caused trouble.
\q1
\v 17 Those evil people eat the bread of wickedness,
\q1 and drink the wine of violence.
\q1
\v 18 In contrast, the path of those who do what is right, is like a bright light,
\q1 →that keeps getting brighter until the brightest time of day.
\q1
\v 19 The path of wicked people is like deep darkness,
\q1 →so they don’t even know what they’re stumbling over.
\b
\q1
\v 20 My child, pay attention to what I’m saying,
\q1 and let my words sink in.
\q1
\v 21 Don’t let them out of your sight,
\q1 and keep them in the centre of your thoughts,
\q1
\v 22 because they’re life to those who find them,
\q1 and healing to their entire bodies.
\q1
\v 23 Guard your mind and thoughts more than anything else,
\q1 →because the way you live all your life, bubbles out from \add your values\add*.
\q1
\v 24 Remove perverse talk from your mouth,
\q1 and keep devious words away from your lips.
\q1
\v 25 Keep your eyes looking towards the front,
\q1 and let your gaze be straight ahead.
\q1
\v 26 Make the path level for your footsteps,
\q1 and let everything you do be straight-forward.
\q1
\v 27 Don’t veer to the left or to the right—
\q1 keep your feet away from evil \add paths\add*.
\c 5
\s1 The warning concerning adultery
\q1
\v 1 My child, pay attention to my wise words,
\q1 and listen carefully to what I’ve learnt
\q1
\v 2 so you won’t lose your discretion,
\q1 and so your lips might guard knowledge.
\q1
\v 3 Although the lips of a forbidden woman drip honey,
\q1 and her speech is smoother than oil,
\q1
\v 4 in the end, she’s as bitter as wormwood,
\q1 and sharper than a double-sided sword.
\q1
\v 5 Her feet are heading down \add towards\add* death,
\q1 and her steps will take her to her grave.
\q1
\v 6 She doesn’t consider the path of life.
\q1 Her paths wander so she doesn’t know where she’ll end up.
\q1
\v 7 So now my children, listen to me,
\q1 and don’t deviate from my advice.
\q1
\v 8 Stay far away from her,
\q1 and don’t go near the entrance to her house,
\q1
\v 9 in case you end up giving your youthful strength to others,
\q1 and your \add best\add* years to a cruel \add person\add*.
\q1
\v 10 Then strangers might make use of your strength \add (as a slave)\add*
\q1 and you \add end up\add* working in a foreigner’s house.
\q1
\v 11 Then you’ll groan when you are about to die
\q1 when your flesh and your body are worn out
\q1
\v 12 and you recall, “How I hated discipline,
\q1 and didn’t like being told what not to do.
\q1
\v 13 I didn’t listen to those who were teaching me,
\q1 and I didn’t take any notice of those who were instructing me.
\q1
\v 14 I was so close to utter calamity,
\q1 and everyone would have noticed me.
\b
\q1
\v 15 Drink water from your own supply\f + \fr 5:15 \ft Many commentators believe that the next several lines are alluding to the marital relationship between a man and his wife.\f*
\q1 and flowing water from your own well.
\q1
\v 16 Should your springs overflow out onto the streets,
\q1 with streams of water in the plaza?
\q1
\v 17 Let them be for you alone,
\q1 and not for any strangers \add who are\add* with you.
\q1
\v 18 May your fountain be blessed,
\q1 and \add may you\add* rejoice in the wife of your youth—
\q1
\v 19 a loving doe, yes, a graceful deer.
\q1 Let her breasts satisfy you always.
\q1 Constantly be overwhelmed by her love.
\q1
\v 20 My son, why would you stagger \add along\add* with a strange woman,
\q1 or embrace the bosom of a foreign woman
\q1
\v 21 because everything a person does is in front of Yahweh’s eyes,
\q1 and he observes all of your decisions?
\q1
\v 22 Wicked people get trapped by their own disobedience,
\q1 and they get tangled in their ropes of rebellion.
\q1
\v 23 They’ll die from a lack of discipline,
\q1 and their own stupidity will lead them astray.
\c 6
\s1 The other still warning
\q1
\v 1 My child, if you \add offer something as\add* a guarantee for your neighbour’s \add loan\add*,
\q1 \add or\add* you’ve shaken hands \add on a deal\add* with a stranger,
\q1
\v 2 \add then\add* you’ve become trapped by your \add own\add* lips—
\q1 you’ve been caught by the words out of your \add own\add* mouth.
\q1
\v 3 In that case, my child, \add go and\add* rescue yourself:
\q1 Since you’ve fallen into your neighbour’s control,
\q1 go \add and\add* humbly appeal to your neighbour \add to release you from that pledge\add*.
\q1
\v 4 Don’t say that you’ll just sleep on it.
\q1 Don’t think that you’ll just have a nap first.
\q1
\v 5 Save yourself \add immediately\add* like a deer when it’s snagged,
\q1 or a bird fleeing from a trapper.
\b
\q1
\v 6 You lazy person, go \add and watch\add* an ant.
\q1 →Think about its behaviour, and be wise.
\q1
\v 7 It doesn’t \add even\add* have a commander,
\q1 \add or\add* overseer or ruler.
\q1
\v 8 It prepares its food in the summer.
\q1 It stores up its supplies in the harvest \add season\add*.
\q1
\v 9 How much longer will you lie there, lazy person?
\q1 When will you get up from your sleep?
\q1
\v 10 A little sleep, a quick nap,
\q1 a little folding of your arms to rest,
\q1
\v 11 and poverty will sneak in like a robber,
\q1 and need \add will threaten\add* like an armed man.
\b
\q1
\v 12 The wicked and worthless man
\q1 walks \add around\add* with a crooked mouth,
\q1
\v 13 winking with his eyes,
\q1 shuffling his feet along,
\q1 and pointing with his fingers.
\q1
\v 14 People like that think perversely—
\q1 always devising evil \add plans\add*
\q1 \add and\add* spreading dissension.
\q1
\v 15 Because of that, disaster will come suddenly—
\q1 they’ll be instantly broken beyond healing.
\q1
\v 16 There are six \add things that\add* Yahweh hates,
\q1 actually seven that he finds detestable.
\q1
\v 17 \add ₁\add* arrogant eyes,
\q1 \add ₂\add* a lying tongue,
\q1 \add ₃\add* hands that shed innocent blood,
\q1
\v 18 \add ₄\add* a mind that devises wicked plans,
\q1 \add ₅\add* feet that rush off to do \add something\add* evil,
\q1
\v 19 \add ₆\add* a dishonest witness that breathes out lies, and
\q1 \add ₇\add* a person that stirs up conflict within the family.
\q1
\v 20 My child, follow \add your father’s\add* commands,
\q1 and don’t abandon what your mother taught you.
\q1
\v 21 Lock them into your mind.
\q1 Tie them around your neck.
\q1
\v 22 When you walk around, what you learnt will guide you.
\q1 When you lie down, it will preserve you.
\q1 When you wake up, it’ll speak to your mind,
\q1
\v 23 because rules are \add like\add* a lamp and teaching \add like\add* a light.
\q1 Rebuke and discipline are the pathway to life
\q1
\v 24 by keeping your from the evil woman—
\q1 from the smooth tongue of a foreign woman.
\q1
\v 25 Don’t lust in your mind for her beauty,
\q1 and don’t let her capture you with her eyelashes,
\q1
\v 26 because the price of a prostitute \add might be as cheap as\add* a loaf of bread,
\q1 ^but \add another\add* man’s wife might cost you your precious life.
\q1
\v 27 Can anyone carry fire against their chest,
\q1 without their clothes catching fire.
\q1
\v 28 If a person walks on coals,
\q1 won’t they get their feet burnt?
\q1
\v 29 It’s like that for anyone sleeping with his neighbour’s wife,
\q1 No one will go unpunished for doing that.
\q1
\v 30 They might not despite a thief
\q1 if they’re stealing to satisfy their hunger,
\q1
\v 31 yet even \add the thief\add* has to pay back seven times as much if they’re caught—
\q1 they might lose everything in their house.
\q1
\v 32 Anyone who commits adultery lacks judgement—
\q1 they \add end up\add* destroying their own life.
\q1
\v 33 They’ll discover pain and dishonour,
\q1 and their shame will never be wiped away,
\q1
\v 34 because jealousy arouses rage in an \add offended\add* person
\q1 who won’t show any compassion when it’s time to avenge.
\q1
\v 35 You won’t just be able to pay them off,
\q1 even if you try to increase the amount.
\c 7
\s1 The woman egpanpanlibug
\q1
\v 1 My child, obey what I’ve told you,
\q1 and store my instructions securely inside you.
\q1
\v 2 Follow my instructions and live,
\q1 and keep my teaching as the focus of your eyes.
\q1
\v 3 Tie them onto your fingers.
\q1 Write them onto your mind.
\q1
\v 4 Tell wisdom that she’s your sister,
\q1 and call understanding a close relative,
\q1
\v 5 so they’ll keep you from the street woman—
\q1 from the ungodly woman whose words sound so smooth.
\q1
\v 6 Once from the window of my house,
\q1 I looked down through the lattice \add slits\add*,
\q1
\v 7 and I saw a simple \add lad\add*—
\q1 a youth lacking much judgement.
\q1
\v 8 He was passing through her street near the corner,
\q1 then he strode along the path to her house.
\q1
\v 9 In the twilight as evening began—
\q1 halfway towards night and darkness.
\q1
\v 10 Then look, a woman greets him,
\q1 dressed like a prostitute and skilled at seduction.
\q1
\v 11 She’s boisterous and defiant,
\q1 and her feet \add don’t know how to\add* stay home.
\q1
\v 12 She’s often on the street or in the plazas,
\q1 and she lies in wait at every corner.
\q1
\v 13 Then she grabbed him and kissed him.
\q1 She looks serious and tells him,
\q1
\v 14 “I have meat at home from my sacrifice \add earlier\add*—
\q1 today I’ve done my religious duties.
\q1
\v 15 That’s why I came out to meet you—
\q1 to look all around until I found you.
\q1
\v 16 I’ve put nice covers on my couch—
\q1 coloured linen fabric from Egypt.
\q1
\v 17 I’ve perfumed my bed
\q1 with \add fragrant\add* myrrh and aloes and cinnamon.
\q1
\v 18 Come \add and\add* let’s enjoy making love until morning.
\q1 We can delight ourselves in our love
\q1
\v 19 because the man of the house is away—
\q1 he’s gone away on a long journey.
\q1
\v 20 He took a bag of money with him.
\q1 He won’t be back home until the middle of the month.”
\q1
\v 21 She persuades him with her seductive talk.
\q1 She compels him with her smooth lips.
\q1
\v 22 He suddenly started following her,
\q1 like a cow going to be slaughtered—
\q1 like a fool with a noose around his neck,
\q1
\v 23 until an arrow pierces his stomach,
\q1 like a bird rushing into a trap,
\q1 not knowing that it will cost him his life.
\b
\q1
\v 24 So listen to me now my children,
\q1 and pay attention to what I’m telling you.
\q1
\v 25 Do let your desires drive you in her direction.
\q1 Don’t wander onto her pathways
\q1
\v 26 because she’s caused many to be pierced and fall—
\q1 it would be difficult to count them all.
\q1
\v 27 Her house is the pathway to the grave—
\q1 descending downwards to deathly rooms.
\c 8
\s1 Praising wisdom
\q1
\v 1 Doesn’t wisdom call out?
\q1 Doesn’t understanding raise her voice?
\q1
\v 2 On the high places by the road,
\q1 she stations herself between the paths.
\q1
\v 3 By the gates at the main entrance to the city,
\q1 she calls out loudly into the doorways.
\q1
\v 4 “To you, people, I call—
\q1 my voice is calling all humankind.
\q1
\v 5 You naive people must learn to be prudent,
\q1 and you foolish people must learn to be discerning.
\q1
\v 6 Listen because I’ll speak uplifting things,
\q1 and open my lips about what is right,
\q1
\v 7 because my mouth will tell the truth,
\q1 and my lips detest wickedness.
\q1
\v 8 All the words in my mouth are honest—
\q1 there’s nothing twisted or crooked among them.
\q1
\v 9 All of them are straightforward to anyone who understands,
\q1 and right to those looking for knowledge.
\q1
\v 10 Choose my instruction over silver,
\q1 and knowledge rather than shiny gold,
\q1
\v 11 because wisdom is better than jewels,
\q1 and there’s nothing more desirable than her.
\q1
\v 12 I, wisdom, live together with good judgement,
\q1 and I’m \add good at finding\add* knowledge and discretion.
\q1
\v 13 Those who strive to obey Yahweh, hate evil,
\q1 I, \add wisdom,\add* hate pride and arrogance,
\q1 evil \add activities\add*, and perverse talk.
\q1
\v 14 Good advice and sound wisdom are mine.
\q1 I have understanding and strength.
\q1
\v 15 I enable kings to reign well,
\q1 and rulers to administer justice.
\q1
\v 16 By me, princes and the leading citizens rule—
\q1 judging with impartiality.
\q1
\v 17 I love those who love me,
\q1 and those who search for me will find me.
\q1
\v 18 Riches and honour accompany me—
\q1 enduring wealth and fairness.
\q1
\v 19 My fruit is better than purest gold,
\q1 and my produce is better than choice silver.
\q2 …
\q1
\v 20 I walk along the road of doing what’s right,
\q1 among the pathways that lead to justice,
\q1
\v 21 so those who love me can inherit wealth,
\q1 as I fill their storerooms.
\b
\q1
\v 22 Yahweh created me among his first actions—
\q1 at the beginning of his works long ago.
\q1
\v 23 I was established from eternity—
\q1 before the beginnings of the world.
\q1
\v 24 I was revealed before the deep oceans existed,
\q1 when there weren’t yet any springs gushing water.
\q1
\v 25 Before the mountains were set into place,
\q1 \add and\add* before the hills were seen, I was revealed.
\q1
\v 26 \add yes, even\add* before he made the earth and fields,
\q1 or the first dust on the ground.
\q1
\v 27 I was there:
\li1 when Yahweh made the skies,
\li1 when he inscribed the \add horizon\add* circle above the oceans,
\li1
\v 28 when he pressed the clouds into shape,
\li1 when he established the springs way down in the oceans.
\li1
\v 29 when he determined the boundaries of the oceans so that the waters wouldn’t go past his line,
\li1 when he marked out the earth’s foundations.
\q1
\v 30 I was beside him as a skilled craftsman—
\q1 He enjoyed my company every day,
\q1 playing in his presence all the time.
\q1
\v 31 I was \add enjoying\add* playing in his world,
\q1 and enjoying humankind’s descendants.
\b
\q1
\v 32 So now my children, listen to me
\q1 because those who follow my ways will find happiness.
\q1
\v 33 Listen when you’re corrected
\q1 and don’t neglect wisdom.
\q1
\v 34 People who listen to me will be blessed
\q1 keeping watch on my doors every day—guarding my doorway,
\q1
\v 35 because anyone who finds me, finds life
\q1 and obtains favour from Yahweh.
\q1
\v 36 Anyone who misses finding me harms themself,
\q1 and those who hate me, love death.
\c 9
\s1 Wisdom versus stupidity
\q1
\v 1 Wisdom has built her house—
\q1 chiselling out its seven pillars.
\q1
\v 2 She’s butchered her meat \add and\add* mixed her wine—
\q1 her table is \add already nicely\add* prepared.
\q1
\v 3 She’s sent out her young women.
\q1 She calls out from the heights in the city.
\q1
\v 4 “Anyone who’s naive, let him turn aside here,”
\q1 she says to the one lacking understanding.
\q1
\v 5 “Come \add and\add* eat my bread,
\q1 and drink the wine I’ve mixed.
\q1
\v 6 Abandon foolish \add ways\add* and live,
\q1 and walk on the path of understanding.
\p
\v 7 Anyone who \add tries to\add* correct a mocker \add just\add* gets insulted,
\q1 and anyone who tells off a wicked person is likely to get hurt.
\q1
\v 8 Don’t \add try to\add* correct a mocker in case he \add just\add* hates you.
\q1 ^Correct a wise person \add on the other hand\add*, and it’ll be appreciated.
\q1
\v 9 Give to a wise person and they’ll become wiser still.
\q1 Inform an honest person and they’ll keep learning.
\q1
\v 10 The first step towards being wise is to strive to obey Yahweh,
\q1 and being aware of the heavenly beings is the beginning of understanding,
\q1
\v 11 because it’s by me that your days will be extended,
\q1 and years will be added to your life.
\q1
\v 12 If you’re wise, you’ll be helping yourself,
\q1 ^and if you mock, then you alone will suffer the result.
\p
\v 13 A foolish woman is boisterous and naive,
\q1 and she doesn’t know anything.
\q1
\v 14 She sits at the doorway of her house,
\q1 \add or\add* on a seat in the city heights,
\q1
\v 15 in order to call out to those who are passing by—
\q1 those \add men\add* who are going straight past on their way.
\q1
\v 16 “Anyone who’s naive can turn in here,”
\q1 she says to those lacking sense.
\q1
\v 17 “Stolen drinks are sweet,
\q1 and secret food tastes good.”
\q1
\v 18 But he doesn’t realise that the dead are there,
\q1 \add or\add* that her guests are in the depths of the grave.
\c 10
\s1 Solomon’s proverbs
\p
\v 1 The proverbs by Solomon:
\q1 A wise son makes his father happy,
\q1 ^but a foolish son causes grief for his mother.
\q1
\v 2 Ill-gotten treasures profit nothing,
\q1 ^but doing the right thing rescues you from death.
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh doesn’t allow those who do the right thing to go hungry,
\q1 ^but he rejects those who are wicked.
\q1
\v 4 Idle hands lead to poverty,
\q1 ^but those who work hard gain wealth.
\q1
\v 5 Anyone who stores their produce in the summer is being sensible,
\q1 ^but the one who sleeps during the harvest is a disgraceful son.
\q1
\v 6 Blessings come to those who do what is right,
\q1 ^but wicked people lie to conceal their violence.
\q1
\v 7 The memory of a godly person is a blessing,
\q1 ^but the name of the wicked person will rot.
\q1
\v 8 The wise person accepts instructions,
\q1 ^but fools will be ruined by their own words.
\q1
\v 9 Anyone who walks with integrity will walk securely,
\q1 ^but \add the person\add* with crooked ways will soon get found out.
\q1
\v 10 The person who winks at you will cause grief,
\q1 and those who speak foolishly will be ruined.
\q1
\v 11 The words from a godly person are a fountain of life,
\q1 ^but the mouth of a wicked person conceals violence.
\q1
\v 12 Hatred stirs up quarrels,
\q1 ^but love covers over all wrongs.
\q1
\v 13 Wisdom is found on the lips of a person with discernment,
\q1 ^but those lacking morals need to be punished.
\q1
\v 14 Wise people store up knowledge,
\q1 ^but the mouth of a fool invites destruction.
\q1
\v 15 The wealth of a rich person gives them security,
\q1 ^\add but\add* poor people are destroyed by their poverty.
\q1
\v 16 The reward of a godly person is life,
\q1 ^\add but\add* a wicked person ends up being punished.
\q1
\v 17 The person who accepts instruction is on the path to life,
\q1 ^but the one who rejects rebuke ends up lost.
\q1
\v 18 Anyone who conceals hatred, has lying lips,
\q1 and anyone who spreads slander is foolish.
\q1
\v 19 A lot of talking doesn’t mean that a person isn’t doing something wrong,
\q1 ^but anyone who keeps their mouth closed is being sensible.
\q1
\v 20 The tongue of a godly person \add adds value like\add* good silver,
\q1 ^but the thoughts of the wicked are of little \add value\add*.
\q1
\v 21 The lips of a godly person shepherd many people,
\q1 ^but foolish people die from the lack of morals.
\q1
\v 22 Yahweh’s blessing leads to prosperity,
\q1 and that’s without adding forced labour to it.
\q1
\v 23 Doing wicked things is like a sport to foolish people,
\q1 ^but a wise person enjoys doing sensible things.
\q1
\v 24 The wicked person’s fears will come to pass,
\q1 ^but the godly person’s desires will be given to them.
\q1
\v 25 When a tornado passes over, it destroys wicked people,
\q1 ^but godly people are \add like\add* an enduring foundation.
\b
\q1
\v 26 Lazy people \add irritate\add* their supervisors
\q1 →like vinegar on their teeth and smoke in their eyes.
\q1
\v 27 Striving to obey Yahweh prolongs your life,
\q1 ^but the years of wicked people will be short.
\q1
\v 28 Godly people anticipate future happiness,
\q1 ^but wicked people will never gain what they expect.
\q1
\v 29 \add Staying on\add* Yahweh’s paths is security for the godly,
\q1 ^but ruin for those who do evil.
\q1
\v 30 Godly people will never be shaken,
\q1 ^but the wicked won’t live long in the land.
\q1
\v 31 The mouth of a godly person produces wisdom as its fruit,
\q1 ^but the tongues of perverse people will be cut off.
\q1
\v 32 The lips of godly person experience pleasure,
\q1 ^but the mouth of the wicked experience perversity.
\c 11
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh hates dishonest scales,
\q1 ^but he delights in an accurate weight
\q1
\v 2 When presumption comes, shame follows,
\q1 ^but modesty is followed by wisdom.
\q1
\v 3 Godly people will be guarded by their integrity,
\q1 ^but treacherous people will be destroyed by their deceit.
\q1
\v 4 Wealth doesn’t help in the day of rage,
\q1 ^but doing what’s right will rescue \add you\add* from death.
\q1
\v 5 \add When\add* an innocent person does what’s right things go straightforwardly,
\q1 ^but the wicked person will fall due to their own wickedness.
\q1
\v 6 The godliness of good people will rescue them,
\q1 ^but treacherous people will be caught out by their own schemes.
\q1
\v 7 When the wicked person dies, all hope is lost,
\q1 and the expectation of strength disappears.
\q1
\v 8 The godly person is rescued from trouble,
\q1 ^but the wicked person gets it instead.
\q1
\v 9 A godless person destroys his neighbour with his mouth,
\q1 ^but the godly will be rescued by knowledge.
\q1
\v 10 When the godly do well, the city celebrates,
\q1 ^and when the wicked perish, there’s a cheerful shout.
\q1
\v 11 A blessing from the upright people helps to elevate a city,
\q1 ^but it gets torn down by the mouth of the wicked.
\q1
\v 12 A person who despises their neighbour lacks good morals,
\q1 ^but a person with understanding will stay quiet.
\q2
\v 13 A gossip goes around revealing secrets,
\q1 ^but a trustworthy person keeps things private.
\q1
\v 14 When there are no directions, a nation falls,
\q1 ^but there’s safety in having lots of advisers.
\q1
\v 15 You’ll end up getting burnt if you guarantee a stranger’s loan,
\q1 ^but avoiding shaking on deals will \add lead to\add* security.
\q1
\v 16 A woman who displays grace to others will obtain honour,
\q1 ^but ruthless people seize wealth.
\b
\q1
\v 17 \add People\add* who display loyal commitment benefit themselves,
\q1 ^but cruel people harm their own selves.
\q1
\v 18 A wicked person can make a living from telling lies,
\q1 ^but someone who sows goodness gets a genuine wage.
\q1
\v 19 True goodness leads to life,
\q1 ^but those who chase after evil, \add receive\add* death.
\q1
\v 20 Yahweh hates those with perverse motives,
\q1 ^but he appreciates those who \add choose\add* blameless ways.
\q1
\v 21 Most certainly, an evil person won’t go unpunished,
\q1 ^but the descendants of a godly person will be rescued.
\q1
\v 22 A beautiful woman without discretion
\q1 →is like a gold ring in a pig’s nose.
\q1
\v 23 Godly people long for good things,
\q1 ^but wicked people can only expect to receive fury.
\q1
\v 24 Someone who helps generously, gains even more,
\q1 ^but those who without what’s due, end up poor.
\q1
\v 25 A person who blesses will prosper,
\q1 and those who give water, will also be satisfied.
\q1
\v 26 People will curse the person who hoards up grain,
\q1 ^but the one who sells it will be blessed by them.
\q1
\v 27 Anyone who strives diligently, strives for favour,
\q1 ^but if you strive for evil, it’ll come to you.
\q1
\v 28 Anyone who trusts in their riches will fall,
\q1 ^but godly people will thrive like green leaves.
\q1
\v 29 Those who cause trouble for their households will inherit the wind,
\q1 and the fool will become a slave to those with sensible values.
\q1
\v 30 The fruit of doing what’s right is a tree of life,
\q1 and those who win people \add over\add* are wise.
\b
\q1
\v 31 Listen, anyone who does what’s right will be rewarded \add appropriately\add*,
\q1 →\add so\add* how more so the wicked and disobedient person!
\c 12
\q1
\v 1 Anyone who loves discipline, loves knowledge,
\q1 ^but those who hate correction are stupid.
\q1
\v 2 A good person receives favour from Yahweh,
\q1 ^but he will condemn the person with wicked plans.
\q1
\v 3 No one can \add become\add* established by means of wickedness,
\q1 ^but the foundations of righteous people can’t be moved.
\q1
\v 4 A capable wife is the crown of her husband,
\q1 ^but a wife who acts shamefully is like rottenness in his bones.
\q1
\v 5 The plans of godly people are \add to do\add* justice,
\q1 ^but wicked people lean towards deceit.
\q1
\v 6 The words of wicked people lie in wait for blood,
\q1 ^but the speech of godly people rescues them.
\q1
\v 7 The wicked get overthrown then disappear,
\q1 ^but the homes of godly people endure.
\q1
\v 8 A person will be praised for their insightful words,
\q1 ^but \add anyone\add* with a warped mind will end up despised.
\q1
\v 9 It’s better to be little known yet have a servant,
\q1 ^than to be self-important but have nothing to eat.
\q1
\v 10 A godly person cares for the life of their animal,
\q1 ^but wicked people \add display\add* cruelty rather than compassion.
\q1
\v 11 The person who tills the ground will have enough food,
\q1 ^but anyone chasing worthless things has the wrong values.
\q1
\v 12 The wicked person covets evil gain,
\q1 ^but godly people produce \add like a deep-rooted tree\add*.
\q1
\v 13 An evil person entices others with their rebellious lips,
\q1 ^but godly people stay out of trouble.
\q1
\v 14 A person will be rewarded with good things from the fruit of their mouth,
\q1 and a person’s labour will be repaid to them \add by its results\add*.
\q1
\v 15 The fools’ paths seem like the right ones in their own eyes,
\q1 ^but a wise person listens to advice.
\q1
\v 16 A fool’s anger is made known straightaway,
\q1 ^but a sensible person overlooks being disrespected.
\q1
\v 17 Anyone who breathes out faithfulness, \add unashamedly\add* says what’s right,
\q1 ^but a biased witness speaks deceitfully.
\q1
\v 18 Some people speak recklessly, like a sword thrashing around,
\q1 ^but wise people bring healing with their words.
\q1
\v 19 Lips that produce reliable words will always be trusted,
\q1 ^but a tongue that spurts out lies \add will be believed\add* only for a moment.
\q1
\v 20 Those who make evil plans, do it from a deceitful mind,
\q1 ^but those who promote peace, \add end up\add* contented.
\q1
\v 21 Trouble doesn’t come towards \add people\add* who do what’s right,
\q1 ^but wicked people are full of distress.
\q1
\v 22 Yahweh hates lying lips,
\q1 ^but he’s pleased by those who are trustworthy.
\q1
\v 23 A sensible person doesn’t show off everything they know,
\q1 ^but foolish people \add like to\add* declare their foolishness.
\q1
\v 24 Those who work hard will rule,
\q1 ^but an idle person will become forced labour.
\q1
\v 25 A person who’s full of anxiety gets weighed down \add by it\add*,
\q1 ^but good news cheers them up.
\q1
\v 26 A godly person keeps an eye on their neighbour,
\q1 ^but the ways of wicked people leads them astray.
\q1
\v 27 A slack hunter doesn’t roast his kill,
\q1 ^but a diligent person values all their possessions.
\q1
\v 28 Life \add can be found by following\add* the path of doing what’s right,
\q1 and there’s no death if \add you stay on\add* the pathway.
\c 13
\q1
\v 1 A wise son \add accepts\add* his father’s discipline,
\q1 ^but a scoffer doesn’t listen to rebuke.
\q1
\v 2 A person can eat well as a result of the fruit of their mouth,
\q1 ^but the appetite of treacherous people is violence.
\q1
\v 3 The person who zips their lips, protects their life,
\q1 ^\add but\add* those who open their mouths, destroy themselves.
\q1
\v 4 Lazy people want a lot but get nothing,
\q1 ^but those who are diligent will have plenty.
\q1
\v 5 Godly people hate fake news,
\q1 ^but the wicked person causes a stink and disgrace.
\q1
\v 6 doing what’s right preserves \add those who like to display\add* integrity,
\q1 ^but wickedness overcomes the sinner.
\q1
\v 7 Some people pretend to be wealthy, but have very little.
\q1 Others act as if they’re poor, but are actually very wealthy.
\q1
\v 8 The rich man’s wealth \add might be needed to\add* ransom his life,
\q1 ^but the poor person hears no threat.
\b
\q1
\v 9 The \add person\add* who does what’s right has a light that shines brightly,
\q1 ^but the lamp of the wicked person gets put out.
\q1
\v 10 Insolence only causes friction,
\q1 ^but those who take advice, \add advance in\add* wisdom.
\q1
\v 11 Wealth gained dishonestly gets frittered away,
\q1 ^but what’s earnt through hard work will increase.
\q1
\v 12 Hoping for something that never happens causes stress,
\q1 ^but when a longing is fulfilled, it’s like a new lease of life.
\q1
\v 13 Anyone who despises instruction will pay for it,
\q1 ^but a person who respects a command will be rewarded.
\q1
\v 14 Instruction from the wise is a fountain of life,
\q1 →turning \add a person\add* away from deadly traps.
\q1
\v 15 Good insight gives favour,
\q1 ^but the path of treacherous people is hard.
\q1
\v 16 Every sensible person makes use of knowledge,
\q1 ^but the fool displays his foolishness.
\q1
\v 17 A wicked messenger gets into trouble,
\q1 ^but reliable messengers help the situation.
\q1
\v 18 Poverty and disgrace are for those who ignore discipline,
\q1 ^but the person who heeds correction will \add end up\add* being honoured.
\q1
\v 19 A desire that gets fulfilled makes \add a person\add* feel pleased,
\q1 ^but foolish people hate to turn away from evil.
\q1
\v 20 \add The person\add* who walks with wise people will become wise,
\q1 ^but anyone associating with fools will get harmed.
\q1
\v 21 Calamity chases after sinners,
\q1 ^but goodness will reward those who do what’s right.
\q1
\v 22 A good person leaves an inheritance for their children,
\q1 ^but a sinner’s wealth will be saved for those who do what’s right.
\q1
\v 23 The unused field of a poor person could produce a lot of food,
\q1 ^but injustice sweeps it away from them.
\q1
\v 24 \add The parent\add* who doesn’t spank their children must hate them,
\q1 ^but the one who loves them, is diligent in disciplining them.
\q1
\v 25 The person who does what’s right will be able to satisfy their appetite,
\q1 ^but the stomach of wicked people doesn’t get enough.
\c 14
\q1
\v 1 The wisest woman builds her house,
\q1 ^but the foolish one tears it down with her own hands.
\q1
\v 2 A good citizen respects and honours Yahweh,
\q1 ^but a devious person despises him.
\q1
\v 3 What comes out of a fool’s mouth is prodded on by pride,
\q1 ^but the lips of wise people protect them.
\q1
\v 4 Where there’s no cattle, the feeding trough will be spotless,
\q1 ^but a good harvest requires the strength of the bull.
\q1
\v 5 A trustworthy witness doesn’t tell lies,
\q1 ^but a biased witness \add just\add* breathes out lies.
\q1
\v 6 A mocker searches for wisdom, but \add finds\add* none,
\q1 ^but knowledge is simple to find for the one who understands.
\q1
\v 7 Go away from where a fool is,
\q1 →otherwise you won’t find knowledgeable lips.
\q1
\v 8 a sensible person has the wisdom to understand their direction,
\q1 ^but fools are deceived by their own foolishness.
\q1
\v 9 Fools mock the idea of feeling guilty,
\q1 →so good citizens favour each other.
\q1
\v 10 A person holds their own bitterness inside,
\q1 and they don’t share their happiness with a stranger \add either\add*.
\q1
\v 11 The wicked person’s house will be destroyed,
\q1 ^but the good citizen’s tent will do well.
\q1
\v 12 There’s a nice path right in front of a person,
\q1 →but there’s death at the end of that road.
\q1
\v 13 People who are laughing can still be aching inside,
\q1 and the end of happiness might be grief.
\q1
\v 14 Someone who turns back on their morals will be satisfied by their ways,
\q1 ^but a good person will be satisfied by what they do.
\q1
\v 15 Naive people believe everything \add they hear\add*,
\q1 ^but smart people \add carefully\add* consider their steps.
\q1
\v 16 A wise person fears trouble and turns away from it,
\q1 ^but a stupid one is careless and overconfident.
\q1
\v 17 People who quickly become angry, act foolishly,
\q1 and those planning evil schemes are hated.
\q1
\v 18 Naive people inherit foolishness,
\q1 ^but clever people are surrounded by knowledge.
\q1
\v 19 Evil people will \add be forced to\add* bow down in front of those who are good,
\q1 and wicked people at the gates of those who do what’s right.
\q1
\v 20 A poor person is hated even by their neighbour,
\q1 ^but there are many who love the rich.
\q1
\v 21 Anyone who despises their neighbour disobeys God,
\q1 ^but the person who is kind to the poor will be happy.
\b
\q1
\v 22 Don’t those who devise evil \add schemes\add* \add end up\add* losing their way?
\q1 ^However, those with loyal commitment and faithfulness devise good things.
\q1
\v 23 Hard work \add results\add* in a profit,
\q1 ^but words from lips only lead to poverty.
\q1
\v 24 The crown of wise people is their wealth.
\q1 ^The foolishness of fools is \add really\add* foolish.
\q1
\v 25 A truthful witness saves lives,
\q1 ^but a deceitful one breathes out lies.
\q1
\v 26 Respecting and obeying Yahweh \add leads to\add* confidence of strength,
\q1 and that will be a safe place for their children.
\q1
\v 27 Respecting and obeying Yahweh is a fountain of life,
\q1 →to turn people from deadly traps.
\q1
\v 28 The pride of a king is \add being over\add* lots of people,
\q1 ^but a shortage of people \add signals\add* the ruin of a ruler.
\q1
\v 29 People who are slow to get angry, have more understanding,
\q1 ^but a quick-tempered person prioritises foolishness.
\q1
\v 30 A peaceful mind \add gives\add* a healthy body,
\q1 ^but envy \add causes\add* the bones to rot.
\q1
\v 31 \add Anyone\add* who oppresses the poor, taunts their maker,
\q1 ^but showing favour to a needy person honours him.
\q1
\v 32 Wicked people destroy themselves by doing evil things,
\q1 ^but those who do what’s right find a safe place in their death.
\q1
\v 33 Wisdom resides in an understanding mind,
\q1 →and it’s made known \add even\add* among foolish people.
\q1
\v 34 Doing what’s right makes a nation great,
\q1 ^but disobeying God \add brings\add* disgrace to people groups.
\q1
\v 35 The king shows favour to a servant who acts with insight,
\q1 ^but his rage is for those who act shamefully.
\c 15
\q1
\v 1 A gentle answer turns fury away,
\q1 ^but a hurtful reply makes people angrier.
\q1
\v 2 A wise person’s tongue advances knowledge,
\q1 ^but the mouth of fools pours out nonsense.
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh’s eyes are everywhere—
\q1 →watching \add both\add* good and evil people.
\q1
\v 4 A soothing tongue is a tree of life,
\q1 ^but perversity crushes the spirit.
\q1
\v 5 Fools reject discipline from their father,
\q1 ^but sensible people accept correction.
\q1
\v 6 There’s great wealth in the home of \add a person\add* who does what’s right,
\q1 ^but the income of a wicked person will \add just\add* be trouble.
\q1
\v 7 The lips of the wise spread knowledge,
\q1 ^but it’s not so for the minds of fools.
\q1
\v 8 The sacrifices of wicked people are hated by Yahweh,
\q1 ^but he delights in the prayer of a godly person.
\q1
\v 9 Yahweh detests what wicked people do,
\q1 ^but he loves those who strive to do what’s right.
\q1
\v 10 Severe discipline is coming for those who abandon the \add right\add* path,
\q1 and those who hate correction will die.
\q1
\v 11 The grave and place of destruction aren’t hidden from Yahweh,
\q1 →so how much more the thoughts and values of humankind.
\q1
\v 12 Mockers never love the one who corrects them,
\q1 →\add and\add* they never approach the wise.
\q1
\v 13 Inner happiness makes a face glad,
\q1 ^but a spirit is crushed by a broken heart.
\q1
\v 14 A discerning mind wants knowledge,
\q1 ^but the mouth of fools feeds on foolishness.
\q1
\v 15 Every day is bad for those who are suffering,
\q1 ^but cheerful people have a continual feast.
\q1
\v 16 It’s better to have a little along with respect for Yahweh,
\q1 ^than having great wealth, and then turmoil with it.
\q1
\v 17 It’s better to have a meal of vegetables where there’s love,
\q1 ^than to have roast meat where there’s hate.
\q1
\v 18 Quick-tempered people stir up trouble,
\q1 ^but a patient person calms a quarrel.
\q1
\v 19 The path of a slacker is like a hedge of thorns,
\q1 ^but the road for godly people is a level highway.
\q1
\v 20 A wise child brings happiness to their father,
\q1 ^but a foolish person despises their mother.
\q1
\v 21 Foolishness brings happiness to those who lack morals,
\q1 ^but an understanding person makes sensible decisions.
\q1
\v 22 Plans go wrong where no one gets advice,
\q1 ^but they get implemented where there’s many advisers.
\q1
\v 23 A person is \add always\add* pleased to give the right answer,
\q1 and the right thing said at the right time is so good.
\q1
\v 24 Life’s path is upward for the wise person,
\q1 →to prevent them going down to the grave.
\q1
\v 25 Yahweh tears down the houses of the proud,
\q1 ^but he’ll protect a widow’s boundary.
\q1
\v 26 Evil plans are hated by Yahweh,
\q1 ^but kind messages are pure.
\q1
\v 27 The \add person\add* who makes unjust gains, brings troubles on their own home,
\q1 ^but the one who hates bribes will live.
\q1
\v 28 The person who does what’s right, thinks carefully before answering,
\q1 ^but the mouth of the wicked pours out evil.
\q1
\v 29 Yahweh is far from the wicked,
\q1 ^but he hears the prayer of those who do what’s right.
\q1
\v 30 A bright look makes others glad.
\q1 Good news makes your bones healthy.
\q1
\v 31 The ear that listens to correction lives—
\q1 →it will remain among the wise people.
\q1
\v 32 Someone who ignores discipline, despises themself,
\q1 ^but anyone who listens to correction strengthens their values.
\q1
\v 33 Wise instructions \add lead to\add* striving to obey Yahweh,
\q1 and humility \add leads to\add* honour.
\c 16
\q1
\v 1 People make their own plans,
\q1 →then Yahweh decides what will happen.
\q1
\v 2 People always tell themselves that they’re doing the right thing,
\q1 ^but Yahweh evaluates their motives.
\q1
\v 3 Entrust what you’re doing to Yahweh,
\q1 →and your plans will be effective.
\q1
\v 4 Yahweh makes everything for his purpose,
\q1 →even the wicked for the day of disaster.
\q1
\v 5 Yahweh detests every person who’s arrogant—
\q1 →they certainly won’t go unpunished.
\q1
\v 6 Disobedience can be made up for by faithfulness and loyal commitment,
\q1 and those who respect and honour Yahweh avoid \add doing what’s\add* evil.
\q1
\v 7 When Yahweh is pleased with a person’s behaviour,
\q1 →he causes even his enemies to be at peace with them.
\q1
\v 8 It’s better to have \add just\add* a little from doing what’s right,
\q1 ^than a large income acquired dishonestly.
\q1
\v 9 People plan their ways in their minds,
\q1 →but Yahweh directs their steps.
\q1
\v 10 Inspired judgements \add come from\add* the king’s lips.
\q1 When making judgements, his mouth must not betray justice.
\q1
\v 11 Honest scales and balances come from Yahweh.
\q1 The weights in the bag are his work.
\q1
\v 12 Wicked behavior is detestable for kings,
\q1 because a throne is established by doing what’s right.
\q1
\v 13 Kings appreciate lips that say what’s right,
\q1 and they value people who tell the truth.
\q1
\v 14 A king’s rage is a messenger of death,
\q1 →and wise people will appease it.
\q1
\v 15 When a king’s face brightens, there’s life.
\q1 His favour is like a rain cloud in the spring.
\q1
\v 16 It’s better to acquire wisdom than gold,
\q1 and to choose understanding rather than silver.
\q1
\v 17 The highway of godly people steers them away from evil.
\q1 Anyone who carefully considers their path, protects their life.
\q1
\v 18 Pride goes before destruction,
\q1 and arrogance before a stumble.
\q1
\v 19 It’s better to \add live\add* humbly among poor people,
\q1 ^than to be dividing up the plunder with the proud.
\q1
\v 20 The person who gives attention to a matter will succeed,
\q1 and those who put their trust in Yahweh will be blessed.
\q1
\v 21 A person with a wise nature will be called ‘understanding’,
\q1 and pleasant lips are more persuasive.
\q1
\v 22 Having insight is \add like\add* a fountain of life,
\q1 ^but disciplining fools would be foolishness.
\q1
\v 23 The wise person’s mind gives insight to their mouth,
\q1 and adds persuasiveness to their lips.
\q1
\v 24 Kind messages are like the honey in a honeycomb,
\q1 →sweet for the self-esteem, and healing for the bones.
\q1
\v 25 There’s a clear path right in front of a person,
\q1 →but its end is the ways to death.
\q1
\v 26 The workers’ appetites work on their behalf,
\q1 →because their hunger presses them on.
\q1
\q2 …
\v 27 A worthless person dredges up scandal,
\q1 →and it’s like scorching fire on their lips.
\q1
\v 28 Perverse people stir up strife,
\q1 →then it separates close friends.
\q1
\v 29 Violent people deceive their neighbours,
\q1 →and lead them on a path that isn’t good.
\q1
\v 30 Those who shut their eyes are planning perverse things.
\q1 People who purse their lips are doing evil.
\q1
\v 31 Gray hair is a crown of splendour,
\q1 that’s found with a life of doing what’s right.
\q1
\v 32 It’s better to be slow to get angry than to be powerful,
\q1 and better \add to be able\add* to control yourself than to capture a city.
\q1
\v 33 The dice are thrown onto the table,
\q1 ^but Yahweh determines their fall.
\c 17
\p
\v 1 It’s better to have a dry piece of bread in quietness,
\q1 ^than a house full of feasting with strife.
\q1
\v 2 A servant who acts wisely will rule over a shameful son,
\q1 →and he’ll share in the inheritance along with the brothers.
\q1
\v 3 There’s a crucible and furnace for gold and silver,
\q1 ^but it’s Yahweh who tests hearts.
\q1
\v 4 Those who do evil pay attention to wicked lips.
\q1 A liar listens to a destructive tongue.
\q1
\v 5 Anyone who mocks the poor, taunts their maker.
\q1 Someone who’s happy about a calamity won’t go unpunished.
\q1
\v 6 Grandchildren are the crown of the aged,
\q1 and children are proud of their parents.
\q1
\v 7 Excellent lips wouldn’t be suitable for a fool,
\q1 \add and\add* certainly deceptive lips don’t suit a ruler.
\q1
\v 8 \add Doing\add* a favour can act like a bribe for the donor.
\q1 →It causes everything they do to prosper.
\q1
\v 9 The person who conceals an offence is seeking acceptance,
\q1 ^but the one repeating the matter separates close friends.
\q1
\v 10 Rebukes work on a sensible person,
\q1 more than a hundred lashes on a fool.
\q1
\q1
\v 11 Only an evil person seeks rebellion
\q1 →and \add so\add* a cruel opponent will be sent against him.
\q1
\v 12 It’s better to meet a \add mother\add* bear robbed of its cubs,
\q1 →than to meet a fool \add displaying\add* their foolishness.
\q1
\v 13 Someone who returns evil for good,
\q1 →\add will find that\add* evil never departs from their house.
\q1
\v 14 The beginning of a dispute is \add like\add* a small water leak,
\q1 →so stop it before it breaks out into a quarrel.
\q1
\v 15 The person who justifies wicked people,
\q1 and the one who condemns an innocent person,
\q1 →are both detestable to Yahweh.
\q1
\v 16 Why would a fool need money in their hand
\q1 when they have no intention of acquiring wisdom.
\p
\v 17 A \add true\add* friend loves at all times,
\q1 and relatives are born \add to help during periods\add* of difficulty.
\q1
\v 18 A person lacking integrity is quick to give a handshake—
\q1 →guaranteeing a loan in front of their neighbour.
\q1
\v 19 The one who loves to disobey, loves contention.
\q1 The person who builds a high fence, invites destruction.
\b
\q1
\v 20 The person with twisted values won’t find goodness,
\q1 and the one with a perverse tongue will descend into disaster.
\q1
\v 21 It’s sad for the parents of a child who makes bad choices,
\q1 and the father of a fool won’t be a happy person.
\q1
\v 22 A cheerful attitude promotes healing,
\q1 ^but a crushed spirit makes your bones dry up.
\q1
\v 23 A wicked person accepts a secret bribe
\q1 →to pervert the paths of justice.
\q1
\v 24 Wisdom is present with the understanding person,
\q1 ^but a fool’s eyes are unable to concentrate.
\q1
\v 25 A foolish child brings grief to his father,
\q1 and bitterness to the mother who gave birth to them.
\q1
\v 26 It’s not good to impose a fine on the innocent.
\q1 Beating up an official isn’t a good thing to do.
\q1
\v 27 A person who’s truly knowledgeable, restrains what they say,
\q1 and an understanding person stays calm.
\q1
\v 28 Even a fool who stays silent is considered to be wise.
\q1 The one with closed lips \add appears to\add* have understanding.
\c 18
\q1
\v 1 People who keep themselves separate, seek their \add own\add* desires,
\q1 They reject sound wisdom.
\q1
\v 2 Fools don’t enjoy learning,
\q1 ^but always like to make their opinions known.
\q1
\v 3 When a wicked person arrives, contempt \add from others\add* also comes,
\q1 and when shame arrives, so does reproach.
\q1
\v 4 The utterances from a person’s mouth are deep waters.
\q1 A fountain of wisdom \add produces\add* a flowing stream.
\q1
\v 5 It’s not good to show partiality to the wicked
\q1 or to deprive an innocent person of justice.
\q1
\v 6 The fool’s lips brings strife,
\q1 and the fool’s mouth invites a beating.
\q1
\v 7 The fool’s mouth \add will bring\add* ruin,
\q1 and the lips of fools are a trap for their minds.
\q1
\v 8 ‘News’ snippets from a gossip are like delicious snacks—
\q1 →they \add get absorbed like food\add* going down into your stomach.
\q1
\v 9 The person who is slack in their work,
\q1 →is a cousin to the one who destroys.
\q1
\v 10 Yahweh’s name is a strong tower.
\q1 →Godly people run into it to be safe.
\q1
\v 11 The rich person’s wealth is \add like\add* a fortified city,
\q1 and in their imagination, like a high wall.
\q1
\v 12 Before destruction \add hits\add*, a person is proud,
\q1 ^but humility precedes honour.
\q1
\v 13 Someone who answers before they’ve \add even\add* listened
\q1 →is being foolish and shameful.
\q1
\v 14 A person’s spirit can endure \add through\add* sickness,
\q1 ^but who can bear a crushed spirit?
\q1
\v 15 Understanding people acquire knowledge,
\q1 and wise people want information.
\q1
\v 16 A person’s donation can make space for them,
\q1 and it will give them access to important people.
\q1
\v 17 The first person to state their case \add sounds\add* right,
\q1 →\add until\add* their opponent cross-examines them.
\b
\q1
\v 18 Tossing a coin ends quarrels,
\q1 →and it settles things between powerful people.
\q1
\v 19 Someone who’s offended \add is as approachable as\add* a fortified city,
\q1 and quarrels are like the bars on a fortress.
\q1
\v 20 The fruit of a person’s mouth satisfies their stomach,
\q1 and they’ll be satisfied with the product of their lips.
\q1
\v 21 The tongue has power over life and death,
\q1 →and those who love \add using\add* it will eat its fruit.
\q1
\v 22 A man who finds a wife, finds a good thing,
\q1 →and receives favour from Yahweh.
\q1
\v 23 Poor people make requests,
\q1 ^but the rich person answers harshly.
\q1
\v 24 A person with many companions \add will\add* come to ruin,
\q1 ^but there is a companion that sticks closer than a brother.
\c 19
\q1
\v 1 It’s better to walk with integrity,
\q1 ^than to be a foolish person with perverse speech.
\q1
\v 2 What’s more, a person without knowledge isn’t good,
\q1 and \add anyone\add* who shifts their feet quickly, misses their way.
\q1
\v 3 A person’s foolishness subverts their course,
\q1 →then their mind rages against Yahweh.
\q1
\v 4 Wealth adds many friends,
\q1 ^but the poor friend ends up being isolated.
\q1
\v 5 A false witness won’t go unpunished,
\q1 and the one who tells lies, won’t escape \add the consequences\add*.
\q1
\v 6 Many people make requests to a generous leader,
\q1 and everyone is the friend of the person who gives gifts.
\q1
\v 7 All the relatives of a poor person hates them,
\q1 →so how much more do their friends avoid them.
\q1 The poor person sends messages after them, but they’re no longer around.
\q1
\v 8 Anyone who acquires integrity, loves their own life.
\q1 The one who preserves understanding will find success.
\q1
\v 9 A lying witness won’t go unpunished,
\q1 and the one who tells lies will die.
\q1
\v 10 Luxury is not suitable for a fool—
\q1 even worse for a slave to rule over princes.
\q1
\v 11 A person’s insight makes them slow to anger,
\q1 and it’s their privilege to overlook an offense.
\q1
\v 12 The king’s rage is like a lion’s roar,
\q1 ^but his favour is like dew on the grass.
\q1
\v 13 A foolish child \add leads to\add* their father’s destruction,
\q1 and an argumentative wife \add is like something\add* constantly dripping.
\q1
\v 14 Houses and wealth are an inheritance from your parents,
\q1 →but a sensible wife is from Yahweh.
\q1
\v 15 Laziness makes \add a person\add* fall into a deep sleep,
\q1 and a slack person will go hungry.
\q1
\v 16 The ones who obey Yahweh’s instructions will preserve their lives.
\q1 ^Anyone who despises his ways, will die.
\q1
\v 17 Someone who’s kind to a poor person is lending to Yahweh,
\q1 →and he will repay for the good deed.
\q1
\v 18 Discipline your child because that gives hope,
\q1 ^but don’t think about their death.
\q1
\v 19 People who get very angry will end up paying a fine,
\q1 →because if you rescue them, you’ll have to do it again and again.
\q1
\v 20 Listen to advise and accept correction
\q1 →so that you’ll become wise for your life ahead.
\q1
\v 21 People make many plans in their minds,
\q1 ^but Yahweh’s advice will endure \add time\add*.
\q1
\v 22 People desire loyal commitment from others,
\q1 and it’s better to have a poor \add friend\add* than \add one\add* who lies.
\q1
\v 23 Respecting and honouring Yahweh leads to life,
\q1 →and \add that person\add* will have \add peaceful\add* nights without trouble arriving.
\q1
\v 24 The lazy person hides their hands in the dish—
\q1 →not even \add troubling\add* to lift it to their mouth.
\q1
\v 25 Beat a mocker then \add that\add* simple one will become sensible,
\q1 and correct an intelligent person and they’ll know even more.
\q1
\v 26 Children who assault their fathers and drive away their mothers
\q1 →bring shame and reproach.
\q1
\v 27 My child, if you want to stray from knowledge,
\q1 →stop listening to correction.
\q1
\v 28 A worthless witness makes a mockery of justice,
\q1 and the mouth of the wicked person gulps in \add more\add* wickedness.
\q1
\v 29 Judgements are prepared for scoffers,
\q1 and beatings for the backs of fools.
\c 20
\q1
\v 1 Wine is a mocker and strong drink makes you boisterous,
\q1 →and everyone who staggers due to it, isn’t wise.
\q1
\v 2 A king’s rage is like a roaring lion.
\q1 →Anyone who provokes him to anger risks their life.
\q1
\v 3 It’s honourable for a person to step away from strife,
\q1 ^but every fool would \add rather\add* break out \add an argument\add*.
\q1
\v 4 A lazy person won’t plough when it’s still cold,
\q1 →then he looks at harvest time and there’s nothing.
\q1
\v 5 The plans in a person’s mind are \add like\add* deep water,
\q1 ^but an understanding person will draw them out.
\q1
\v 6 Many people talk about their loyalty,
\q1 ^but who can \add actually\add* find a faithful person.
\q1
\v 7 Godly people act with integrity.
\q1 →Their children will be blessed after them.
\q1
\v 8 A king sitting on his throne to make a judgement,
\q1 →sifts all evil out with his eyes.
\q1
\v 9 Who can say, “I’ve kept my motives pure—
\q1 I’ve been freed from sin”?
\q1
\v 10 Differing weights and unequal measures—
\q1 →Yahweh hates both of them.
\q1
\v 11 Even young people are known by their actions
\q1 →if they’re honest and obedient.
\q1
\v 12 The ear that hears and the eye that sees—
\q1 →it was Yahweh that made both of them.
\q1
\v 13 Don’t love sleep or you might end up in poverty.
\q1 Open your eyes then you’ll have enough food.
\q1
\v 14 “Worthless, worthless,” say the buyers,
\q1 ^but as soon as they’ve left, they boast \add about the good deal\add*.
\q1
\v 15 There’s gold \add around\add*, and plenty of jewels,
\q1 ^but lips with knowledge are a rare commodity.
\q1
\v 16 Take the person’s coat if they’ve used it to guarantee the loan of a stranger,
\q1 and hold it in pledge if they did it for a foreign woman.
\q1
\v 17 The bread of lies tastes good \add at first\add*,
\q1 ^but afterwards it’s \add like\add* a mouthful of gravel.
\q1
\v 18 Plans are established with advisers,
\q1 →so wise guidance \add is required for\add* war.
\q1
\v 19 The person who gossips reveals a secret,
\q1 →so don’t get involved \add with someone\add* with a big mouth.
\q1
\v 20 The one who curses their father and mother,
\q1 →will have their lamp extinguished in deep darkness.
\q1
\v 21 An inheritance that’s gained at too young an age,
\q1 →won’t be a blessing in the end.
\q1
\v 22 Don’t say, “I’ll repay \add that\add* evil.”
\q1 →Wait for Yahweh and he’ll rescue you.
\q1
\v 23 Fiddling with the weights is hated by Yahweh,
\q1 and deceitful scales aren’t good.
\q1
\v 24 It’s Yahweh that determines a person’s steps,
\q1 →so how does a \add mere\add* human understand their course?
\q1
\v 25 It’s a trap for a person to rashly declare something as sacred,
\q1 →and then to \add only\add* reflect \add later\add* after making vows.
\q1
\v 26 A wise king winnows out the wicked \add to separate them\add*.
\q1 He uses the thresher \add to break them off\add*.
\q1
\v 27 The human spirit is Yahweh’s lamp,
\q1 →searching all the innermost parts.
\q1
\v 28 Loyal commitment and faithfulness to king preserves him,
\q1 and his throne is upheld by \add that\add* loyal commitment.
\q1
\v 29 The splendour of young men is their strength,
\q1 and the adornment of old people is their grey hair.
\q1
\v 30 Lashes and wounds clean out evil,
\q1 and beatings clean out the innermost parts.
\c 21
\q1
\v 1 The king’s thoughts are like streams of water to Yahweh—
\q1 →he directs them to go wherever he wants.
\q1
\v 2 All a person’s ways seem right in their own eyes,
\q1 ^but Yahweh evaluates motives.
\q1
\v 3 To do what’s right and fair
\q1 →is more desirable to Yahweh than sacrificing.
\q1
\v 4 Arrogance and pride—the lamp of wicked people,
\q1 →is disobeying \add Yahweh\add*.
\q1
\v 5 The plans of diligent people will only \add lead\add* to abundance,
\q1 ^but those who \add just\add* rush around will only \add get\add* poverty.
\q1
\v 6 Wealth that’s acquired via a lying tongue,
\q1 →will be like a \add quick\add* breath for those seeking death.
\q1
\v 7 The violence of wicked people will drag them away,
\q1 because they refuse to \add apply\add* justice.
\q1
\v 8 The paths of a guilty person are crooked,
\q1 ^but well-behaved people act with decency.
\q1
\v 9 It’s better to live in the corner of the attic\f + \fr 21:9 \ft We made an adjustment here for most of our readers in houses with sloping roofs, as distinct from living in an arid climate and having a flat roof.\f*
\q1 ^than inside the \add main\add* house with the company of an argumentative wife.
\q1
\v 10 Wicked people have a strong, inner craving for evil.
\q1 →\add Even\add* their neighbours won’t be shown any favour.
\q1
\v 11 By punishing a mocker, a naive person becomes wise,
\q1 and when wise people are instructed, they acquire knowledge.
\q1
\v 12 People who do what’s right pay attention to the wicked person’s house,
\q1 →subverting the wicked until they’re ruined.
\q1
\v 13 Any person who shuts their ears to the cry of the poor
\q1 →will also call out \add for help\add* but won’t be answered.
\q1
\v 14 A discreet gift subdues anger,
\q1 and a bribe quietens down a strong rage.
\q1
\v 15 The godly person enjoys promoting justice,
\q1 ^but \add brings\add* terror to those doing wicked things.
\q1
\v 16 Anyone who wanders \add away\add* from the path of insight,
\q1 →will rest in the assembly of the dead.
\q1
\v 17 The lover of pleasure will become poor.
\q1 Anyone who loves wine and rich food won’t become wealthy.
\q1
\v 18 A wicked person becomes the payment to free someone who does what’s right,
\q1 and a treacherous person to free a godly person.
\q1
\v 19 It’s better to live in a wilderness region,
\q1 ^than with a wife who’s argumentative and negative.
\q1
\v 20 Precious treasures and oil will be in the house of a wise person,
\q1 ^but a foolish person consumes it \add all\add*.
\q1
\v 21 The person who strives to do what’s right and who \add shows\add* loyal commitment,
\q1 →will find life, forgiveness, and honour.
\q1
\v 22 A wise man can climb up into a city of warriors,
\q1 →and bring down the strength of its confidence.
\q1
\v 23 Any person who guards their mouth and tongue
\q1 →will keep trouble away from their life.
\q1
\v 24 A proud and arrogant person is called a mocker,
\q1 →\add because\add* they act with arrogant presumption.
\q1
\v 25 The craving of slackers will kill them,
\q1 because their hands refuse to work.
\q1
\v 26 All day long, they fantasise their craving,
\q1 ^but people who do what’s right, give without holding back.
\q1
\v 27 The wicked person’s sacrifice is detestable,
\q1 because it’s brought with evil intent.
\q1
\v 28 A lying witness will die,
\q1 ^but a \add good\add* listener will speak out success.
\q1
\v 29 The wicked person makes their face look stern,
\q1 ^but godly people \add carefully\add* consider their ways.
\q1
\v 30 There’s no wisdom or understanding
\q1 or advice that would oppose Yahweh.
\q1
\v 31 A horse is prepared for the day of battle,
\q1 ^but the victory belongs to Yahweh.
\c 22
\q1
\v 1 A good reputation is more desirable than great wealth,
\q1 and good favour more than gold or silver.
\q1
\v 2 Rich and poor meet each other \add at their end\add*.
\q1 Yahweh is the creator of all of them.
\q1
\v 3 A prudent person notices trouble and avoids it,
\q1 whereas naive people just blunder onwards and suffer.
\q1
\v 4 The consequence of humility and respecting and honouring Yahweh
\q1 →\add is\add* riches and honour and life.
\q1
\v 5 Thorns \add and\add* traps are on the perverse person’s path,
\q1 ^but those who guard their \add own\add* self-esteem keep far away from them.
\q1
\v 6 Train your children in the way they should go,
\q1 →then when they’re older they won’t turn away from it.
\b
\q1
\v 7 A rich person rules over those who are poor,
\q1 and the one who borrows is enslaved to the lender.
\q1
\v 8 The person who sows evil will harvest trouble,
\q1 and the power from his anger will end.
\q1
\v 9 Generous people will be blessed,
\q1 because they share their food with the poor.
\q1
\v 10 Drive away a mocker and then strife will disappear,
\q1 →and abuse and disputes will stop.
\q1
\v 11 The person who values pure motives, who speaks graciously,
\q1 →will have the king for a friend.
\q1
\v 12 Yahweh’s eyes guard knowledge,
\q1 ^but he overthrows the plans of those who behave treacherously.
\q1
\v 13 The lazy person says, “There’s a lion outside—
\q1 →I’ll be killed if I go outside!”
\q1
\v 14 The mouth of a loose woman is a deep pit.
\q1 →Those who are cursed by Yahweh will fall into it.
\q1
\v 15 Foolish ideas are natural in the mind of a youth.
\q1 →\add but\add* the rod bringing discipline drives it far from them.
\q1
\v 16 Those who oppress the poor or give to the rich, in order to make a profit,
\q1 →will \add just\add* end up in poverty \add themselves\add*.
\s1 The thirty sayings
\p
\v 17 Listen carefully to what wise people have said.
\q1 Set your mind to apply what I’m teaching you
\q1
\v 18 because internalising my instructions will be worthwhile—
\q1 →\add then\add* they’ll be easy for you to share with others.
\q1
\v 19 I’ll instruct you today
\q1 →so that \add you’ll\add* put your trust in Yahweh.
\q1
\v 20 Actually, I’ve written thirty proverbs
\q1 to advise and teach you—
\q1
\v 21 to show you true and reliable principles
\q1 →so that you can give accurate answers to \add those\add* sent to you.
\s2 -1-
\p
\v 22 Don’t rob those in poverty just because they’re poor
\q1 and don’t ‘crush’ a poor person at the \add community\add* gate
\q1
\v 23 →because Yahweh will take their side.
\q1 \add Yes,\add* he’ll plunder the lives of those who plunder them.
\s2 -2-
\p
\v 24 Don’t associate with angry people,
\q1 and don’t go along with a \add hot-headed person\add*,
\q1
\v 25 →in case you end up copying their habits,
\q1 and get yourself tangled in \add that\add* trap.
\s2 -3-
\p
\v 26 Don’t be among those quick to shake hands
\q1 to guarantee the loans \add of others\add*,
\q1
\v 27 because if you’re unable to make the payment,
\q1 →you could have your bed taken out from under you.
\s2 -4-
\p
\v 28 Don’t move an ancient boundary \add marker\add*,
\q1 which your ancestors had placed there.
\s2 -5-
\p
\v 29 You’ve seen a man who’s skilled at his work—
\q1 →he’ll take his position in front of kings.
\q1 ^He won’t do his work \add just\add* for anybody.
\q1
\c 23
\s2 -6-
\p
\v 1 When you sit down to eat with a political leader,
\q1 pay attention to what’s in front of you,
\q1
\v 2 and put a knife to your throat,
\q1 if you have an enormous appetite.
\q1
\v 3 Don’t crave their delicacies,
\q1 because it’s the food of lies.
\s2 -7-
\p
\v 4 Don’t \add just\add* work to get rich.
\q1 Your wisdom \add should tell you to\add* stop.
\q1
\v 5 You glance at wealth then it’ll be gone,
\q1 because it’ll make wings for itself and \add fly away into\add* the sky like an eagle.
\s2 -8-
\p
\v 6 Don’t eat the food of a stingy person,
\q1 and don’t crave for their delicacies,
\q1
\v 7 because they’re always calculating internally—
\q1 telling you to eat and drink but it’s not really genuine.
\q1
\v 8 You’ll vomit up the bit that you’ve eaten
\q1 and your pleasant greetings will have been wasted.
\s2 -9-
\p
\v 9 Don’t \add bother\add* speaking to a foolish person
\q1 because they’ll despise the wise things that you say.
\s2 -10-
\p
\v 10 Don’t move an ancient boundary marker,
\q1 or encroach on the fields of those who are disadvantaged,
\q1
\v 11 because the one who watches out for them is strong
\q1 and he’ll take up their case against you.
\q1
\s2 -11-
\p
\v 12 Make continual learning part of your values,
\q1 and use your ears for \add gaining\add* knowledge.
\s2 -12-
\p
\v 13 It’s important to discipline your children.
\q1 They won’t die from getting a spanking.
\q1
\v 14 Spank them with a stick
\q1 and you’ll keep them from the grave.
\s2 -13-
\p
\v 15 My child, if you value wisdom
\q1 then I’ll certainly be very happy.
\q1
\v 16 My mind will be celebrating
\q1 when your lips speak out what’s right.
\s2 -14-
\p
\v 17 Don’t let yourself \add start\add* envying sinners—
\q1 instead it should be Yahweh that you respect and strive to obey,
\q1
\v 18 because then there’ll certainly be a future.
\q1 Your hope for the future won’t be chopped off.
\s2 -15-
\p
\v 19 My child, listen and become wise,
\q1 and channel your values in the right direction.
\q1
\v 20 Don’t associate with those drinking too much wine,
\q1 or with those who gorge themselves on meat,
\q1
\v 21 →because drunkards and gluttons will become impoverished,
\q1 and their drowsiness will leave them dressed in rags.
\s2 -16-
\p
\v 22 Listen to what your father \add tells you\add*,
\q1 and don’t despise your mother when she gets old.
\q1
\v 23 Acquire truth and don’t sell it,
\q1 and \add similarly for\add* wisdom, instruction, understanding.
\q1
\v 24 The father of someone who does what’s right will be encouraged.
\q1 Yes, anyone who fathers a sensible child will be glad.
\q1
\v 25 Let your father be happy.
\q1 and may your mother celebrate.
\s2 -17-
\p
\v 26 My child, follow me with sincerity,
\q1 and let your eyes observe my ways,
\q1
\v 27 because a prostitute is a deep pit,
\q1 and an adulteress is a narrow well.
\q1
\v 28 They lie in wait like a robber would,
\q1 and keep adding to the number of men who become unfaithful.
\s2 -18-
\p
\v 29 Who has dread or sorrow or contentions?
\q1 Who has complaints, or needless wounds, or bloodshot eyes.
\q1
\v 30 \add It’s\add* those who linger over wine,
\q1 or try various mixed drinks.
\q1
\v 31 Don’t look at wine when it’s red and sparkling in the cup,
\q1 \add and when\add* it would slide down smoothly.
\q1
\v 32 →In the end it bites like a snake,
\q1 and stings like a viper.
\q1
\v 33 →\add Then\add* your eyes will see strange things,
\q1 and perverse things in your mind will get spoken.
\q1
\v 34 →You’ll feel like someone sleeping out in the ocean—
\q1 just like someone lying down at the top of a mast.
\q1
\v 35 \add You’ll say,\add* “They hit me \add but\add* it didn’t hurt.
\q1 They beat me \add but\add* I couldn’t feel it.
\q1 When will I wake up? I’ll have some more—where is it?”
\c 24
\s2 -19-
\p
\v 1 Don’t be envious of evil men,
\q1 and don’t try to associate with them,
\q1
\v 2 because they have violent thoughts,
\q1 and their lips cause trouble.
\s2 -20-
\p
\v 3 It’s through wisdom that a house is built,
\q1 and it’s established by understanding.
\q1
\v 4 It’s through knowledge that its rooms get filled
\q1 →with beautiful and valuable treasures.
\s2 -21-
\p
\v 5 A wise person is strong,
\q1 and those with knowledge increase their power,
\q1
\v 6 →because you need wise guidance to go to war,
\q1 and victory requires many advisers.
\s2 -22-
\p
\v 7 Wisdom is too much for fools.
\q1 →They don’t open their mouths at the \add city\add* gate.
\s2 -23-
\p
\v 8 The person who plans to do evil,
\q1 will be known by others as a master schemer.
\s2 -24-
\p
\v 9 A foolish plan is sinful,
\q1 and people hate a mocker.
\s2 -24-
\p
\v 10 If you display a lack of courage when you have troubles,
\q1 →your strength is limited.
\s2 -25-
\p
\v 11 Rescue those being led away to their death,
\q1 and those who will be staggering to their slaughter if you restrain.
\q1
\v 12 If you \add just\add* say that you didn’t know anything about it,
\q1 →then the one who weighs motives will realise.
\q1 →Then the one who guards your life will repay everyone according to what they’ve done.
\s2 -26-
\p
\v 13 My child, eat honey because it’s good,
\q1 and honeycomb tastes sweet.
\q1
\v 14 Wisdom is like that for your mind,
\q1 and if you find it, there’ll be a future for you,
\q1 and what you hope for won’t be taken away from you.
\s2 -27-
\p
\v 15 Don’t lie in wait like a wicked person does at a good person’s home.
\q1 Don’t devastate the place where a good person lives,
\q1
\v 16 because a good person might fall several times then get up \add again\add*,
\q1 ^but the wicked stumble \add when\add* calamity \add comes\add*.
\s2 -28-
\p
\v 17 Don’t cheer when your enemy falls,
\q1 and when they stumble, don’t celebrate
\q1
\v 18 →because Yahweh will see and disapprove,
\q1 →and turn his anger away from them.
\s2 -29-
\p
\v 19 Don’t get yourself worrying because of evil people.
\q1 Don’t be jealous of the wicked,
\q1
\v 20 →because there’s no future for an evil person.
\q1 The lamp of the wicked will be extinguished.
\s2 -30-
\p
\v 21 My child, honour Yahweh and the king and strive to obey them,
\q1 Don’t get involved with those who change \add sides\add*
\q1
\v 22 →because disaster will suddenly hit them
\q1 and who knows what ruin they both might bring \add then\add*?
\s1 More wise sayings
\p
\v 23 These are more sayings of the wise:
\p It’s not good to show partiality when deciding a case.
\q1
\v 24 Anyone who says to the guilty that they’re innocent,
\q1 →will be cursed by the peoples and hated by the nations.
\q1
\v 25 However, those who convict the guilty will be admired,
\q1 →and they’ll be blessed by good things \add happening\add*.
\p
\v 26 An honest answer \add is like\add* a kiss on the lips.
\p
\v 27 Establish your work for outsiders and prepare your food gardens
\q1 then after that, build your house.
\p
\v 28 Don’t be a witness against your neighbour without good reason,
\q1 or deceive others with what you say.
\q1
\v 29 Don’t say, “I’ll do to him what he did to me,
\q1 I’ll pay him back for what he’s done.”
\p
\v 30 I passed by the field of a lazy person
\q1 and by the vineyard of a person with no initiative.
\q1
\v 31 Wow, thorns had grown up over all of it,
\q1 and it was \add completely\add* covered with weeds,
\q1 and its stone walls were broken down.
\q1
\v 32 I looked and made a decision.
\q1 I saw it and learnt from it:
\q1
\v 33 A little sleep, a quick nap,
\q1 a little folding your arms to rest,
\q1
\v 34 and poverty will sneak up on you like a robber,
\q1 and need will approach like an armed bandit.
\c 25
\s1 More of Solomon’s sayings
\p
\v 1 There are also these other proverbs from Solomon that the men \add working for\add* Judah’s King Hezekiah copied:
\p
\v 2 It is God’s privilege to conceal a matter,
\q1 ^and the king’s privilege to try to discover a matter.
\p
\v 3 The skies are high and the earth \add goes\add* deep,
\q1 →and the motives of kings are unable to be predicted.
\p
\v 4 Remove the dross from silver,
\q1 →and then a container comes from the silversmith’s \add hands\add*.
\q1
\v 5 Remove \add the\add* wicked person from the king’s presence,
\q1 →and then his rule will be established as being honest and fair.
\p
\v 6 Don’t promote yourself in front of a king,
\q1 and don’t claim a place among the well-known people,
\q1
\v 7 because it’s better if they tell you, “Come up here,”
\q1 ^than to be placed lower \add right\add* in front of a prince as you look at him.
\p
\v 8 Don’t be in a rush to take someone to court.
\q1 →What would you do at the end, if your neighbour put you to shame?
\p
\v 9 Sort out your case with your neighbour,
\q1 without revealing other people’s secrets,
\q1
\v 10 in case someone hears you and puts you to shame
\q1 and your bad reputation never gets repaired.
\p
\v 11 Good advice given at the right time
\q1 is \add like\add* gold apples set in a silver \add frame\add*.
\p
\v 12 A wise rebuke to a listening ear
\q1 is \add like\add* a gold earring and a fine-gold ornament.
\p
\v 13 To those who send him, a faithful messenger
\q1 is like cool snow when doing a day’s hard work,
\q1 and it refreshes the soul of his masters.
\p
\v 14 A person who boasts about a gift that never comes
\q1 is \add like\add* clouds and wind without rain.
\p
\v 15 A ruler can be persuaded by being patient,
\q1 and a soft tongue can break a bone.
\p
\v 16 \add When\add* you find honey, eat just what you need,
\q1 otherwise you might vomit if you’ve had too much.
\p
\v 17 Don’t set foot in your neighbour’s house too often,
\q1 in case they get fed up with you and \add end up\add* hating you.
\p
\v 18 A man who gives a false testimony against his neighbour,
\q1 is like a club, or a sword, or a sharp arrow.
\p
\v 19 Having confidence in someone who acts treacherously in a time of trouble,
\q1 is \add like having\add* a broken tooth, or \add having\add* your foot slip.
\p
\v 20 Taking off your coat on a cold day,
\q1 \add is like pouring\add* vinegar on baking soda
\q1 or singing songs to someone who’s feeling sad.
\p
\v 21 Give your enemy food to eat if they’re hungry,
\q1 and water to drink if they’re thirsty,
\q1
\v 22 because you’re heaping burning coals on their head,
\q1 and Yahweh will \add be the one\add* to repay you.
\p
\v 23 The northerly wind brings rain \add with it\add*,
\q1 and a gossipping tongue brings indignant looks.
\p
\v 24 If better to live in the corner of an attic,
\q1 than to live inside the house with a contentious wife.
\p
\v 25 Good news from a distant country,
\q1 is \add like\add* cool water for a tired person.
\p
\v 26 A godly person giving in to someone wicked,
\q1 is \add like\add* a spring that gets muddied, or a well that gets ruined.
\p
\v 27 It isn’t good to eat too much honey,
\q1 and nor is trying to make yourself look good.
\p
\v 28 A person who has no self-control,
\q1 is \add like\add* a city where the walls have been broken down.
\c 26
\q1
\v 1 Like snow in the summer, or rain at harvest time,
\q1 so \add too\add* honour isn’t fitting for a fool.
\q1
\v 2 Like a fluttering bird or a darting swallow,
\q1 so \add too\add* an undeserved curse won’t land on you.
\q1
\v 3 \add There’s\add* a whip for the horse and a bridle for the donkey,
\q1 and a rod \add to beat\add* the fool’s back.
\q1
\v 4 Don’t answer a fool with a foolish answer,
\q1 otherwise you’ll \add just\add* end up like them.
\q1
\v 5 Answer a fool according to their foolish thinking,
\q1 so they don’t think they’re cleverer than they are.
\q1
\v 6 Sending a fool with your message to pass on
\q1 is \add like\add* cutting off your feet and drinking in violence.
\q1
\v 7 The legs of a lame person \add just\add* dangle down,
\q1 \add like\add* a proverb in the mouth of fools.
\q1
\v 8 Giving honour to a fool,
\q1 is like tying the stone into the sling.
\q1
\v 9 A drunkard getting thorns in his hand,
\q1 is \add like\add* a fool trying to explain a proverb.
\q1
\v 10 Hiring a fool or an \add unknown\add* passer-by,
\q1 is \add like\add* an archer just randomly shooting arrows.
\q1
\v 11 A fool who keeps doing the same foolish things,
\q1 is like a dog that returns to its vomit.
\q1
\v 12 Do you see a person who thinks they’re clever?
\q1 There’s more hope for a fool than for that person.
\q1
\v 13 The lazy person says, “There’s a young lion on the road.
\q1 There’s a lion out there on the streets.”
\q1
\v 14 The door turns on its hinges,
\q1 and the slacker turns in their bed.
\q1
\v 15 The sluggard keeps their hand in the dish—
\q1 they’re too lazy to bring it back to their mouth.
\q1
\v 16 The slacker is wiser in their own eyes,
\q1 ^than seven people who give good advice.
\q1
\v 17 The person who gets angry about a quarrel that’s nothing to do with them,
\q1 is \add like\add* someone grabbing the ears of a dog that’s passing by.
\q1
\v 18-19 A person who deceives their neighbour then says they were just joking
\q1 is like a madman shooting flaming arrows.
\q1
\v 20 When there’s no more firewood, the fire goes out,
\q1 and when there’s no gossiper, a conflict dies down.
\q1
\v 21 \add Like\add* wood \add gets the\add* fire burning
\q1 and charcoal \add keeps the\add* embers hot,
\q1 →so too a contentious person kindles strife.
\q1
\v 22 The words of a gossip are like delicious morsels,
\q1 and they go right down into the person’s insides.
\q1
\v 23 Burning lips and evil motives
\q1 are \add like\add* a silver overlay on a clay pot.
\q1
\v 24 A person who hates can disguise it with their lips,
\q1 ^but they’re accumulating deceit inside.
\q1
\v 25 When they say favourable things, don’t trust them,
\q1 because there’s seven detestable things in within them.
\q1
\v 26 Hatred can be covered by deception,
\q1 →\add but\add* it will be uncovered when people gather together.
\q1
\v 27 The person who digs a pit will fall into it,
\q1 and the one who rolls a stone will have it come back on them.
\p
\v 28 A lying tongue despises those who are crushed by it,
\q1 ^and a flattering mouth leads to calamity.
\c 27
\q1
\v 1 Don’t boast about \add what will happen\add* tomorrow,
\q1 because you don’t know what the next day will bring.
\q1
\v 2 Let some other person praise you, rather than your own mouth—
\q1 someone from out of town and not your own lips.
\q1
\v 3 Stones are heavy, and sand weighs you down,
\q1 →but being provoked by a fool is heavier than either of them.
\q1
\v 4 Anger is overwhelming and rage is cruel,
\q1 →but who can tolerate jealousy?
\q1
\v 5 Open rebuke is better than concealed love.
\q1
\v 6 Wounds from a friend are faithful,
\q1 ^but kisses from an enemy are confusing.
\q1
\v 7 Honey isn’t so attractive when you’re full,
\q1 ^but even bitter \add food\add* is sweet when you’re hungry.
\q1
\v 8 A person who wanders away from their home,
\q1 is like a bird that wanders from its nest.
\q1
\v 9 Lotions and perfumes make a person cheery,
\q1 →but it’s so pleasant to be given good advice by a friend.
\q1
\v 10 Don’t abandon your friends or your father’s friends,
\q1 and don’t go to your brother’s house when calamity hits you.
\q1 A friend nearby is better than a brother living a long way away.
\q1
\v 11 My child, make me happy by becoming wise,
\q1 →and then I’ll be able to answer anyone who taunts me.
\q1
\v 12 A sensible person sees evil and stays out of sight
\q1 ^\add but\add* naive people continue on then get punished.
\q1
\v 13 If someone guarantees a stranger’s loan, then \add definitely\add* take their coat,
\q1 or if they stand as guarantee for a foreign woman.
\q1
\v 14 If someone blesses their neighbour using a loud voice early in the morning,
\q1 →it will be considered as a curse.
\q1
\v 15 A contentious woman is like a continual dripping on a rainy day,
\q1
\v 16 Restraining her is \add like\add* stopping the wind,
\q1 or \add like\add* catching something oily with one hand.
\q1
\v 17 Iron sharpens iron,
\q1 and one neighbour sharpens another.
\q1
\v 18 The caretaker of a fig tree can eat its fruit,
\q1 and \add someone\add* protecting his master should be honoured.
\q1
\v 19 As water \add reflects\add* your face,
\q1 so too, a person’s behaviour \add reflects\add* their values.
\q1
\v 20 The grave and destruction are never satisfied,
\q1 and people’s eyes are never satisfied.
\q1
\v 21 There’s a smelting pot for silver, and a furnace for gold,
\q1 but a person is refined by the praise they receive.
\q1
\v 22 Even if you could pound a fool like grain in a mortar,
\q1 →their foolishness can’t be separated from them.
\p
\v 23 \add Make sure\add* you know the state of your flock well.
\q1 Set your mind on your \add cattle\add* herds,
\q1
\v 24 because wealth \add doesn’t last\add* forever,
\q1 and a crown isn’t passed down to every generation.
\p
\v 25 The grass dries off and then fresh grass appears,
\q1 and then the herbs are gathered from the mountains.
\q1
\v 26 The lambs will \add be for\add* your clothing,
\q1 and the male goats will become the price for a field.
\q1
\v 27 The female goats will give enough milk for your household,
\q1 and nourishment for your servant girls.
\c 28
\q1
\v 1 The wicked run away when no one’s even chasing them,
\q1 ^but the godly person is as confident as a lion.
\q1
\v 2 A country in rebellion has many rulers,
\q1 ^but an understanding man who knows what’s right will last long.
\q1
\v 3 Someone who’s poor but oppresses \add other\add* poor people,
\q1 is \add like\add* driving rain when you have nothing to eat.
\q1
\v 4 Those who’ve abandoned the law, praise the wicked,
\q1 ^but those who keep the law strive against them.
\q1
\v 5 Evil people don’t understand justice,
\q1 ^but those who strive after Yahweh, understand everything.
\q1
\v 6 A poor person with integrity
\q1 ^is better than someone who’s rich but crooked.
\q1
\v 7 A sensible child follows Yahweh’s instructions,
\q1 ^but those who carouse with gluttons, shame their father.
\q1
\v 8 The person who increases their wealth by charging interest and by overcharging,
\q1 gathers it but it’ll end up going to someone who’s kind to the poor.
\q1
\v 9 A person who turns their ear away from hearing Yahweh’s instructions,
\q1 →even their prayers are detestable.
\q1
\v 10 The one who leads good people astray will fall into their own pit,
\q1 ^but those who are blameless will inherit good.
\q1
\v 11 Rich people see themself as wise,
\q1 ^but a poor person with understanding, sees right through them.
\q1
\v 12 When godly people triumph, there’s great celebration,
\q1 ^but when it’s the wicked, people hide.
\q1
\v 13 Someone covering their faults won’t prosper,
\q1 ^but the one who confesses and then abandons their bad behaviour, will be shown mercy.
\q1
\v 14 The person who strives to allows obey Yahweh will be blessed,
\q1 ^but the one who firmly resists will fall into trouble.
\q1
\v 15 A wicked ruler over struggling people,
\q1 is \add like\add* a lion roaring or a bear attacking.
\q1
\v 16 A ruler lacking understanding \add can become\add* an oppressor.
\q1 The person who hates dishonest gain will have a longer life.
\q1
\v 17 A man tormented by the killing of another will flee to the pit.
\q1 Don’t let anyone support him.
\q1
\v 18 The person with blameless behaviour will be rescued,
\q1 ^but the crooked one will suddenly fall.
\q1
\v 19 The person working their land will have enough food,
\q1 ^but those wasting their time will have their fill of poverty.
\q1
\v 20 A faithful person will have great blessing,
\q1 ^but the one trying to get rich quick won’t go unpunished.
\q1
\v 21 It’s not good to show partiality,
\q1 yet a person will break the rules for a piece of bread.
\q1
\v 22 Those who rush to get wealthy are greedy,
\q1 ^but they don’t realise that poverty will come to them.
\q1
\v 23 If you correct another person, you’ll be appreciated in the end
\q1 more than someone who just says what they wanted to hear.
\q1
\v 24 People who rob their parents and say they’ve done nothing wrong
\q1 are in the same class as those who are destructive.
\q1
\v 25 Greedy people stir up strife,
\q1 ^but \add the one\add* who trusts Yahweh will prosper.
\q1
\v 26 The person who trusts their own plans is a fool,
\q1 ^but \add the one\add* who walks with wisdom will be rescued.
\q1
\v 27 Those who give to the poor will have all they need,
\q1 ^but \add the one\add* who \add just\add* looks away will receive many curses.
\q1
\v 28 When wicked people come to power, everyone hides,
\q1 ^but when they die, godly people increase.
\c 29
\q1
\v 1 The person who gets rebuked but \add just\add* stiffens their neck,
\q1 will be broken suddenly and beyond recovery.
\q1
\v 2 When it’s the godly ones who increase, the people are happy,
\q1 ^but when it’s the wicked, a nation groans.
\q1
\v 3 Those who love wisdom, make their parents glad,
\q1 ^but \add the one\add* who spends time with prostitutes, destroys wealth.
\q1
\v 4 A king establishes the country with justice,
\q1 ^but one who accepts bribes, tears it down.
\q1
\v 5 Someone who flatters their neighbour
\q2 is spreading out a net to capture them.
\q1
\v 6 The disobedience of an evil person will trap them,
\q1 ^but the godly person sings and celebrates.
\q1
\v 7 A godly person understands the poor person’s \add court\add* case,
\q1 ^but the wicked person doesn’t understand knowledge.
\q1
\v 8 Mockers inflame a city,
\q1 ^but wise people deflect anger.
\q1
\v 9 \add If\add* a wise person takes a fool to court,
\q1 then there’ll be rage and ridicule, but no resolution.
\q1
\v 10 People who’ve killed hate those who are blameless,
\q1 ^but good citizens try to protect them .
\q1
\v 11 The fool vents all their anger,
\q1 ^but a wise person holds it back.
\q1
\v 12 A ruler who pays attention to lies,
\q1 will \add only\add* have wicked officials.
\q1
\v 13 A poor person and someone who’s oppressed have lots in common
\q1 ^but it’s Yahweh who gives hope to both of them.
\q1
\v 14 A king who judges the poor fairly,
\q1 will always rule securely.
\q1
\v 15 A scolding and a spanking give wisdom,
\q1 ^but an undisciplined child will bring shame to their mother.
\q1
\v 16 When the wicked increase, crime increases,
\q1 ^but those who do what’s right will \add live to\add* see their downfall.
\q1
\v 17 Discipline your child and you’ll be able to relax,
\q1 and they’ll bring you happiness.
\q1
\v 18 When there’s no vision, the people have no boundaries,
\q1 ^but those who follow Yahweh’s instructions will be happy.
\q1
\v 19 A servant can’t be corrected \add just\add* with words,
\q1 because \add even\add* if they understand, they won’t answer.
\q1
\v 20 \add If\add* a person is quick to speak out,
\q1 there’s more hope for a fool than for them.
\q1
\v 21 Someone who’s pampered their servants since they were young,
\q1 \add can only expect\add* insolence in the end.
\q1
\v 22 An angry person stirs up strife,
\q1 and someone who rages causes big trouble.
\q1
\v 23 A person’s pride will bring them down,
\q1 ^but a humble person will be honoured.
\q1
\v 24 Someone who helps a thief, hates their own life.
\q1 They take the oath yet refuse to testify.
\q1
\v 25 A person’s fear becomes a trap,
\q1 ^but the one who trusts Yahweh will be kept safe.
\q1
\v 26 Many people want to plead to the ruler,
\q1 ^but it’s Yahweh who gives a person justice.
\q1
\v 27 those who do what’s right detest corrupt people,
\q1 ^but \add on the other hand\add*, the wicked detest good citizens.
\c 30
\s1 Agur’s sayings
\p
\v 1 \add These are\add* the sayings by Yakeh’s son Agur, the burden, told to Itiel and Ukal:
\q1
\v 2 I’m certainly among the stupid people
\q1 and lack the understanding that most people have,
\q1
\v 3 and I haven’t been well educated,
\q1 ^but I do know about the holy ones.
\q1
\v 4 Who’s been up to heaven and come back down again?
\q1 Who’s captured the wind between their two hands?
\q1 Who’s wrapped the oceans around them like a cloak?
\q1 Who’s set up all the earth’s boundaries?
\q1 What’s his name, and what’s the name of his son?
\q1 Surely you know?
\p
\v 5 Every message from God is flawless.
\q1 He protects those who go to him to be safe.
\q1
\v 6 Don’t add to his messages
\q1 in case he rebukes you and you’re found to be a liar.
\s1 More sayings
\p
\v 7 \add There’s\add* two things that I want from you—
\q1 don’t keep them from me before I die:
\q1
\v 8 Keep deceitfulness and lying far away from me,
\q1 and don’t give me either poverty or riches—
\q1 \add just\add* give me the food that I need
\q1
\v 9 because if I had plenty, I might deny \add God\add*, asking, “Who is Yahweh?”
\q1 and if I became poor, I might steal and dishonour God’s reputation.
\p
\v 10 Don’t make false accusations to a slave’s master,
\q1 in case he curses you and you have to bear the guilt.
\p
\v 11 \add There’s a\add* generation that curses their fathers
\q1 and doesn’t bless their mothers.
\p
\v 12 \add There’s a\add* generation who are pure in their own eyes,
\q1 ^but they’re not \add actually\add* washed from their filthiness.
\p
\v 13 \add There’s a\add* generation with very proud eyes,
\q1 and their eyebrows are \add also\add* raised.
\p
\v 14 \add There’s a\add* generation whose teeth are swords,
\q2 and its fangs are knives
\q1 to eliminate from the earth those who are suffering,
\q2 and the needy from humankind.
\p
\v 15 The leech has two daughters: Give! Give!”
\p There are three, maybe four, things that are never satisfied—
\q1 four that never have enough:
\li1
\v 16 \add 1.\add* the grave,
\li1 \add 2.\add* a childless woman,
\li1 \add 3.\add* land that never has enough water, and
\li1 \add 4.\add* fire that never stops spreading.
\p
\v 17 The eye that mocks its father,
\q1 and scorns its mother instead of obeying,
\q1 will be pecked out by the ravens in the valley,
\q1 and the young vultures will eat it.
\p
\v 18 There’s three things that are too wonderful for me,
\q1 \add maybe\add* four that I don’t understand:
\li1
\v 19 \add 1.\add* how an eagle’s soars in the sky,
\li1 \add 2.\add* how a snake slithers over a rock,
\li1 \add 3.\add* how a ship sails across the ocean, and
\li1 \add 4.\add* how a young man loves a young woman.
\p
\v 20 This is the way of a woman who commits adultery:
\q1 She eats and wipes her mouth,
\q1 and declares that she hasn’t done anything wrong.
\p
\v 21 There’s three things that makes the earth tremble—
\q1 four things it can’t handle:
\li1
\v 22 \add 1.\add* a slave becoming king,
\li1 \add 2.\add* a fool who has plenty to eat,
\li1
\v 23 \add 3.\add* a hated woman who gets married, and
\li1 \add 4.\add* a female slave who supplants her mistress.
\p
\v 24 There are four creatures on the earth
\q1 that are small but extremely wise:
\li1
\v 25 \add 1.\add* Individual ants aren’t strong, but they store up food in the summer.
\li1
\v 26 \add 2.\add* Rock badgers aren’t very powerful, but they make their homes in the cliffs.
\li1
\v 27 \add 3.\add* Locusts don’t have a king, but they go out in formation \add like soldiers\add*.
\li1
\v 28 \add 4.\add* You can catch a lizard in your hands, yet they get into king’s palaces.
\p
\v 29 There are three things that take fancy steps—
\q1 \add maybe\add* four that walk impressively.
\li1
\v 30 \add 1.\add* the lion who’s a powerful animal that doesn’t turn back from facing anything,
\li1
\v 31 \add 2.\add* the strutting rooster,
\li1 \add 3.\add* male goats, and
\li1 \add 4.\add* a king with his bodyguards around him.
\p
\v 32 If you’ve been foolish enough to brag about yourself,
\q1 then plan to put your hand over your mouth,
\q1
\v 33 because churning milk makes butter,
\q1 squeezing the nose makes it bloody,
\q1 and stirring up anger produces strife.
\c 31
\s1 King Lemuel’s sayings
\p
\v 1 \add These are\add* the sayings by King Lemuel, the burden, that his mother had taught him:
\p
\v 2 \add Now\add* what my son? My son that I gave birth to.
\q1 The son from my marriage.
\q1
\v 3 Don’t give your strength to women,
\q1 or use your intelligence to destroy kings.
\q1
\v 4 Lemuel, it’s not for kings to drink wine
\q1 or for rulers to crave strong drink,
\q1
\v 5 in case they drink and forget their own laws
\q1 and \add end up\add* depriving the poor of justice.
\q1
\v 6 Give strong drink to those who are dying,
\q1 and wine to those who are bitterly depressed.
\q1
\v 7 They’ll drink it then forget their poverty,
\q1 and not remember their misery any more.
\p
\v 8 Open your mouth for those who can’t speak—
\q1 for the cause of all those who are dying.
\q1
\v 9 Open your mouth \add to\add* promote justice,
\q1 and to defend the poor and needy.
\s1 The good spouse woman
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\p
\v 10 Who can find a capable wife,
\q1 →and she’ll be worth far more than jewels?
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\p
\v 11 Her husband trusts her completely,
\q1 →and he doesn’t lack in prosperity.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\p
\v 12 She doesn’t harm him, but does him good,
\q1 right throughout her life.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\p
\v 13 She searches for wool and flax,
\q1 and works eagerly with her hands.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\p
\v 14 She’s like \add those\add* merchant ships—
\q1 she brings her food from far away.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\p
\v 15 She gets up when it’s still dark
\q1 to provide food for her household
\q1 and her servant girls.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\p
\v 16 She appraises a field and buys it.
\q1 Using her own earnings, she plants a vineyard.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\p
\v 17 She keeps herself fit,
\q1 and her arms are strong.
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\p
\v 18 She’s aware that her merchandise is quality.
\q1 Her lamp doesn’t go out at night.
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\p
\v 19 Her hands work the spindle—
\q1 \add spinning thread for cloth\add*.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\p
\v 20 She’s generous to the poor,
\q1 and reaches out to the needy.
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\p
\v 21 She’s not worried about her household when it snows,
\q1 because they’re all dressed in quality clothes.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\p
\v 22 She makes blankets for herself,
\q1 fine linen, and her purple clothing.
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\p
\v 23 Her husband is well-known in the community,
\q1 where he sits with the nation’s elders.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\p
\v 24 She makes linen clothes and sells them,
\q1 and she supplies sashes to the merchants.
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\p
\v 25 She’s clothed with strength and dignity,
\q1 and she laughs as she thinks about the future.
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\p
\v 26 It’s always wisdom that comes out of her mouth,
\q1 and loyal commitment \add guides\add* the instructions on her tongue.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\p
\v 27 She keeps an eye on the \add comings and\add* goings of her household,
\q1 and she doesn’t indulge in laziness.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\p
\v 28 Her sons stand and compliment her,
\q1 and her husband \add also\add* praises her:
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\p
\v 29 “There’s many women with ability,
\q1 ^but you’re better than any of them.”
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\p
\v 30 Charm is deceptive, and beauty is fleeting,
\q1 ^but a woman that honours and strives to obey Yahweh will be praised.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\p
\v 31 Give her the honour that she deserves,
\q1 and let her abilities praise her at the \add city\add* gates.
